CHAPTER I GENERAL PROVISIONS

Article 1 (Purpose) The purpose of this Act is to contribute to the strengthening of agricultural competitiveness, the balanced development of the national economy, and the preservation of national land based on the stabilization of farmers’ agricultural management and the improvement of agricultural productivity through the efficient utilization and management of farmland by prescribing matters necessary for ownership, utilization, preservation, etc. of farmland.

Article 2 (Definitions) The terms used in this Act are defined as follows: <Amended on Dec. 21, 2007; Apr. 1, 2009; May 27, 2009; Dec. 24, 2018; Aug. 17, 2021; Jan. 2, 2024; Feb. 27, 2026>
  1. "Farmland" means any of the following pieces of land:
  a. A rice field, a field, an orchard, and other land actually used as farmland for crops, or land for cultivating perennial plants prescribed by Presidential Decree, regardless of the legal land category; provided, any land prescribed by Presidential Decree, including grassland created under the Grassland Act, is excluded herefrom;
  b. Site for facilities prescribed by Presidential Decree as improvement facilities and production facilities for agricultural and stock farm products installed on the land referred to in item a;
  c. Site for facilities prescribed by Presidential Decree, such as restrooms and parking lots, that are installed on the land referred to in item a and are convenience facilities necessary for farming operations;
  2. "Farmer" means any individual prescribed by Presidential Decree who is engaged in agriculture;
  3. "Agricultural corporation" means an agricultural partnership established under Article 16 of the Act on Fostering and Supporting Agricultural and Fisheries Business Entities, and an agricultural company incorporated under Article 19 of that Act and at least 1/3 of the persons having executive authority of which are farmers:
  a. Deleted; <May 27, 2009>
  b. Deleted; <May 27, 2009>
  4. "Agricultural management" means that a farmer or an agricultural corporation conducts agricultural business on his or her or its own account and responsibility;
  5. "Self-cultivation" means that a farmer continuously engages in the cultivation of crops or the growing of perennial plants on farmland owned by that farmer, or cultivates or grows such crops or plants using his or her own labor for not less than 1/2 of the farming operations, and that an agricultural corporation cultivates crops or grows perennial plants on farmland owned by the corporation;
  6. "Entrusted management" means a form of agricultural management in which the owner of farmland entrusts all or part of the farming operations to a third party by agreeing with him or her to pay a certain remuneration therefor;
  6-2. "Farmland improvement" means any of the following activities to change the form and quality of farmland for purposes of enhancing the productivity of farmland:
  a. Rearranging farmland parcels or installing improvement facilities to increase the utilization value of farmland;
  b. Dressing, filling or cutting soil, or extracting rocks on the relevant farmland for the improvement of soil or for better irrigation, drainage and use of agricultural machinery;
  7. "Farmland conversion" means that farmland is used for purposes other than for agricultural production or improvement of farmland, such as the cultivation of crops or the growing of perennial plants; provided, where the farmland is used for purposes prescribed in subparagraph 1b or c, it shall not be deemed conversion;
  8. "Weekend or experiential farming" means that an individual who is not a farmer cultivates crops or grows perennial plants as a hobby or leisure activity on the weekend, etc.

Article 3 (Basic ideology concerning farmland) (1) Since farmland is the foundation necessary for supplying food to Korean citizens and for preserving the national environment, and is a valuable finite resource influencing the harmonious development of agriculture and the national economy, it shall be carefully preserved and properly managed for public welfare and the exercise of the rights to farmland shall entail necessary restrictions and obligations.
  (2) Farmland shall be owned and used in a manner that enhances agricultural productivity, and shall not become an object of speculation.

Article 4 (Obligations of the State) (1) The State and local governments shall devise and execute farmland-related policy measures so that the basic ideology concerning farmland may be realized.
  (2) When formulating farmland-related policy measures, the State and local governments shall endeavor to contribute to the fostering of agriculture and the balanced development of the national economy by ensuring that farmland can be preserved and reasonably utilized through necessary regulations and adjustment.

Article 5 (Obligations of citizens) Every citizen shall respect the basic ideology concerning farmland and cooperate in implementation of policy measures concerning farmland by the State and local governments.

CHAPTER II OWNERSHIP OF FARMLAND

Article 6 (Restrictions on farmland ownership) (1) Farmland shall be owned only by a person who uses or will use it for his or her own agricultural management.
  (2) Notwithstanding paragraph (1), farmland may be owned in any of the following cases; provided, the farmland owned shall be used for agricultural management (excluding subparagraphs 2 and 3): <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008; Dec. 29, 2008; May 27, 2009; Jun. 9, 2009; Jan. 17, 2012; Dec. 18, 2012; Mar. 23, 2013; May 29, 2016; Oct. 31, 2017; Feb. 11, 2020; Apr. 13, 2021; Aug. 17, 2021; Jun. 16, 2026>
  1. Where the farmland is owned by the State or a local government;
  2. Where the farmland is acquired and owned, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs, by a school under the Elementary and Secondary Education Act or the Higher Education Act, or by a public organization, agricultural research institution, agricultural producer organization, or a producer of seeds and seedlings or of other agricultural machinery and materials, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs, to be used as a site necessary for experiments, research, practice, production of seeds and seedlings, or production of pollen used for artificial pollination of fruit trees so that he or she or it conducts his or her or its intended project;
  3. Where the farmland, other than that in an agricultural promotion area under Article 28, is owned for weekend or experiential farming;
  4. Where the person acquires and owns the farmland by inheritance (including a testamentary gift to an inheritor; hereinafter the same shall apply);
  5. Where the person retains the ownership of the farmland owned at the time he or she ceased farming even after he or she, who had conducted agricultural management for a period longer than that prescribed by Presidential Decree, ceased farming;
  6. Where the person acquires and owns mortgaged farmland under Article 13(1) (including where a special purpose company, etc. as defined in Article 3 of the Asset-Backed Securitization Act acquires farmland from any of the mortgagees prescribed in Article 13(1)1 through 4);
  7. Where the farmland is owned by a person who has obtained permission for farmland conversion under Article 34(1) (including any authorization, permission, approval, etc. which is legally construed as permission for farmland conversion under other statutes), or a person who has filed a report on farmland conversion under Article 35 or 43;
  8. Where the person owns farmland, the consultation on conversion of which under Article 34(2) has been complete;
  9. Where the person acquires and owns farmland less than 1,500 square meters as prescribed by Presidential Decree in a farmland development project district under Article 24(2) of the Korea Rural Community Corporation and Farmland Management Fund Act, or farmland under Article 98(3) of the Agricultural and Fishing Villages Improvement Act;
  9-2. Where the person owns farmland outside an agricultural promotion area referred to in Article 28, the average inclination rate from the uppermost to the lowest parts of which is at least 15 degrees, as prescribed by Presidential Decree;
  10. In any of the following cases:
  a. Where the Korea Rural Community Corporation established under the Korea Rural Community Corporation and Farmland Management Fund Act (hereinafter referred to as the "Korea Rural Community Corporation") acquires and owns farmland pursuant to that Act;
  b. Where farmland is acquired and owned pursuant to Article 16, 25, 43, 82 or 100 of the Agricultural and Fishing Villages Improvement Act;
  c. Where reclaimed farmland is acquired and owned pursuant to the Public Waters Management and Reclamation Act;
  d. Where farmland is acquired and owned by land expropriation;
  e. Where farmland is acquired and owned pursuant to the Act on Acquisition of and Compensation for Land for Public Works Projects following consultation with the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs;
  f. Where farmland in a planned control area or green-belt area designated under Article 36 of the National Land Planning and Utilization Act, the reservation of which is deemed necessary by the Public Land Reservation Deliberation Committee established under Article 7(1) of the Public Land Reservation Act, out of land defined in subparagraph 1a of Article 2 of the same Act, is acquired and owned by the Korea Land and Housing Corporation. In such cases, the acquired farmland shall be leased or lent free of charge, without delay, after being entrusted to the Korea Rural Community Corporation until it is converted for other purposes.
  (3) Notwithstanding paragraph (1) or (2), where a person leases farmland or allows it to be used free of charge pursuant to Article 23(1)1 through 7, the person may continue to own the farmland during the period for which it is leased or allowed to be used free of charge. <Amended on Feb. 11, 2020; Apr. 13, 2021; Jun. 16, 2026>
  (4) Except as permitted in this Act, no special case concerning the ownership of farmland shall be prescribed.

Article 7 (Maximum limit of farmland ownership) A person who intends to engage in weekend farming or experiential farming may own farmland with a total area of less than 1,000 square meters. In such cases, the area shall be calculated based on the aggregate area of farmland owned by all members of the household.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Jun. 16, 2026]

Article 7-2 (Prohibited activities) No person shall engage in any of the following activities:
  1. Recommending or arranging the ownership of farmland, even with knowledge of any violation of restrictions on farmland ownership under Article 6 or the maximum limit of farmland ownership under Article 7;
  2. Recommending or arranging the entrusted management of farmland, even with knowledge of any violation of restrictions on the entrusted management of farmland under Article 9;
  3. Recommending or arranging the lease or gratuitous loan for use of farmland, even with knowledge of any violation of restrictions on the lease or loan for use of farmland under Article 23;
  4. Advertising any activity referred to in subparagraphs 1 through 3 and a business place in which such activity takes place.
  [This Article Added on Aug. 17, 2021]

Article 8 (Issuance of qualification certificates for acquisition of farmland) (1) Any person who intends to acquire farmland shall obtain a qualification certificate for acquisition of farmland from the head of a Si (referring to the head of a Si which does not have Gus and, in the case of a Si combined with forms of urban and rural communities, referring to cases where the location of the farmland is in the same Si only), the head of a Gu (in the case of a Gu of a Si combined with forms of urban and rural communities, referring to cases where the location of the farmland is in the same Gu only), or the head of a Eup or Myeon (hereinafter referred to as the "head of a Si/Gu/Eup/Myeon") having jurisdiction over the location of the farmland; provided, in any of the following cases, a person may acquire farmland without obtaining a qualification certificate for acquisition of farmland: <Amended on May 27, 2009>
  1. Where acquiring farmland under Article 6(2)1, 4, 6, 8, or 10 (excluding item f of the same subparagraph);
  2. Where acquiring farmland by merger of agricultural corporations;
  3. Where acquiring farmland due to the division of co-owned farmland, or due to other reasons prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  (2) A person who intends to obtain a qualification certificate for acquisition of farmland under paragraph (1) shall prepare an agricultural management plan or a plan for weekend or experiential farming containing each of the following matters and file an application for the issuance of the certificate with the head of the Si/Gu/Eup/Myeon having jurisdiction over the location of the farmland, attaching the documents prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs; provided, a person who acquires farmland under Article 6(2)2, 7, 9, 9-2, or 10f may file an application for the issuance of such certificate without preparing an agricultural management plan or a plan for weekend or experiential farming or without attaching the documents prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs: <Amended on May 27, 2009; Aug. 17, 2021; Aug. 16, 2023>
  1. The area of the farmland to be acquired (where the farmland is intended to be acquired for co-ownership, the percentage of each share of the farmland and the location of the farmland to be acquired by each person shall also be indicated);
  2. A plan for securing labor force, agricultural machinery, equipment, and facilities required for the agricultural management of the farmland to be acquired;
  3. The utilization status of the farmland owned (only applicable to an owner of farmland);
  4. The occupation and farming career of a person who intends to obtain a qualification certificate for acquisition of farmland and the farming distance to the farm land;
  (3) Where a person prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs, such as a person who intends to acquire farmland in an area where farmland speculation is or is likely to be widespread, files an application for the issuance of a qualification certificate for acquisition of farmland, the head of the Si/Gu/Eup/Myeon shall have such application undergo deliberation by the relevant farmland committee under Article 44. <Added on Aug. 17, 2021>
  (4) Upon receipt of an application for the issuance of a qualification certificate for acquisition of farmland under paragraph (1), the head of the Si/Gu/Eup/Myeon shall issue such certificate to the relevant applicant within seven days (four days where a person is eligible to file an application for the issuance of a qualification certificate for acquisition of farmland without preparing an agricultural management plan or a plan for weekend or experiential farming pursuant to the proviso of paragraph (2); and 14 days where such application is subject to deliberation by the relevant farmland committee pursuant to paragraph (3)) from the date of receipt of the application. <Added on Aug. 17, 2021; Aug. 16, 2023>
  (5) Matters necessary for applications and procedures for issuance under the main clause of paragraph (1) and paragraph (2) and other matters shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on Aug. 17, 2021>
  (6) When a person who acquires farmland after having obtained a qualification certificate for acquisition of farmland under the main clause of paragraph (1) and paragraph (2) applies for registration of the farmland ownership, he or she shall attach thereto the qualification certificate for acquisition of farmland. <Amended on Aug. 17, 2021>
  (7) Except as provided in this Article, the treatment of civil petitions related to the issuance of a qualification certificate for acquisition of farmland shall be governed by the Civil Petitions Treatment Act. <Added on Aug. 17, 2021>

Article 8-2 (Period of retention of agricultural management plan) (1) The head of a Si/Gu/Eup/Myeon shall retain an agricultural management plan or a plan for weekend or experiential farming as submitted under Article 8(2) for 10 years. <Amended on Aug. 16, 2023>
  (2) The period of retaining documents for filing an application for a qualification certificate for acquisition of farmland, other than an agricultural management plan or a plan for weekend or experiential farming, shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on Aug. 16, 2023>
  [This Article Added on Feb. 11, 2020]

Article 8-3 (Restrictions on issuance of qualification certificates for acquisition of farmland) (1) Where a person who intends to obtain a qualification certificate for acquisition of farmland fails to specify the matters to be included in an agricultural management plan or a plan for weekend or experiential farming or to submit documents to be attached pursuant to Article 8(2), the head of a Si/Gu/Eup/Myeon shall not issue a qualification certificate for acquisition of farmland to such person.
  (2) Where the number of persons who intend to acquire one parcel of farmland for co-ownership exceeds the number prescribed by ordinance of a Si/Gun/Gu pursuant to Article 22(3), the head of a Si/Gu/Eup/Myeon need not issue a qualification certificate for acquisition of farmland.
  (3) Where an agricultural partnership or agricultural company is deemed to meet the requirements for asking the court to issue an order for dissolution pursuant to Article 20-3(2) of the Act on Fostering and Supporting Agricultural and Fisheries Business Entities based on a fact-finding survey, etc. conducted under Article 20-2 of the same Act, the head of a Si/Gu/Eup/Myeon need not issue a qualification certificate for acquisition of farmland.
  [This Article Added on Aug. 17, 2021]

Article 9 (Entrusted management of farmland) An owner of farmland shall not entrust the management of his or her own farmland to another person except in any of the following cases:
  1. Where conscripted or called pursuant to the Military Service Act;
  2. Where traveling abroad for at least 3 months;
  3. Where an agricultural corporation is in liquidation;
  4. Where self-cultivation is impossible due to disease, school attendance, taking public office by election, or other reasons prescribed by Presidential Decree;
  5. Where the management of farmland is entrusted according to the implementation plan for the farmland utilization promotion project under Article 17;
  6. Where a farmer entrusts part of his or her farming operations to another person due to a lack of his or her own labor.

Article 10 (Disposal of farmland not used for agricultural management) (1) Where an owner of farmland falls under any of the following subparagraphs, he or she shall dispose of the relevant farmland (in the case of subparagraph 6, farmland corresponding to the area exceeding the maximum limit on farmland ownership) within 1 year from the date on which the relevant ground arises; provided, this shall not apply to farmland located within a areas subject to permission designated pursuant to Article 10(1) of the Act on Report on Real Estate Transactions (limited to farmland owned after obtaining a certificate of qualification for farmland acquisition under Article 8(1) or obtaining permission for a land transaction contract pursuant to Article 11 of the Act on Report on Real Estate Transactions; hereinafter referred to as "farmland within an area subject to permission of land transaction"): <Amended on May 27, 2009; Mar. 23, 2013; Feb. 11, 2020; Aug. 17, 2021; Aug. 16, 2023; Jun. 16, 2026>
  1. Where an owner fails to use his or her farmland for his or her own agricultural management without good cause prescribed by Presidential Decree, including natural disasters, farmland improvement, and diseases, or where the head of a Si (referring to a Si which has no Gu; hereafter the same shall apply in this Article)/Gun/Gu has recognized as such;
  2. Where 3 months have passed since an agricultural company owning the farmland failed to meet the requirements referred to in subparagraph 3 of Article 2;
  3. Where the head of a Si/Gun/Gu has recognized that a person who acquired the farmland under Article 6(2)2 ceases to use the farmland for the relevant intended project;
  4. Where the head of a Si/Gun/Gu has recognized that a person who acquired the farmland under Article 6(2)3 ceases to use the farmland for weekend or experiential farming without good cause prescribed by Presidential Decree, including natural disasters, farmland improvement or diseases;
  4-2. Where a person who has acquired and owns farmland pursuant to Article 6(2)4 is not using, or is deemed by the head of a Si/Gun/Gu to have ceased using, the farmland for his or her own agricultural management without good cause prescribed by Presidential Decree, such as leasing the farmland by entrusting it to the Korea Rural Community Corporation pursuant to Article 23(1)7;
  4-3. Where a person who owns farmland pursuant to Article 6(2)5 is not using, or is deemed by the head of a Si/Gun/Gu to have ceased using, the farmland for his or her own agricultural management without good cause prescribed by Presidential Decree, such as leasing the farmland by entrusting it to the Korea Rural Community Corporation pursuant to Article 23(1)7;
  5. Where a person who acquired the farmland pursuant to Article 6(2)7 has not commenced the intended project within 2 years from the date of acquisition of such farmland;
  5-2. Where a person owns the farmland without finishing consultation with the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs under Article 6(2)10e;
  5-3. Where a person fails to, without delay, entrust his or her farmland to the Korea Rural Community Corporation under Article 6(2)10f;
  6. Where it has been proved that the owner of farmland owns the farmland in excess of the maximum limit of farmland ownership determined under Article 7;
  7. Where the head of a Si/Gun/Gu has recognized that the owner of farmland fails to implement the details of his or her agricultural management plan or plan for weekend or experiential farming under Article 8(2) without good cause prescribed by Presidential Decree, including natural disasters, farmland improvement, or diseases.
  (2) The head of a Si/Gun/Gu shall notify the owner of farmland who becomes subject to an obligation to dispose of farmland under paragraph (1) that he or she shall dispose of the farmland, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs, specifying information on the farmland subject to disposal and the period of obligation for disposal, etc. <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008; Mar. 23, 2013>
  (3) An owner of farmland who is required to dispose of farmland pursuant to paragraph (1) shall dispose of the farmland subject to disposition to a person who does not fall under any of the following subparagraphs: <Added on Jun. 16, 2026>
  1. A member of the household who shares the household with the owner as of the date a cause falling under any of the subparagraphs of paragraph (1) occurs;
  2. The spouse or a lineal ascendant or descendant of the owner;
  3. A corporation or organization represented by the owner;
  4. Where the owner is a corporation, a person specially related to the corporation under Article 542-8(2)5 of the Commercial Act.
  (4) Where the head of a Si/Gun/Gu gives notice pursuant to paragraph (2), he or she shall report the fact of such notice to the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs through the Special Metropolitan City Mayor, Integrated Special Metropolitan City Mayor, Metropolitan City Mayor, Special Self-Governing City Mayor, Do Governor, or Special Self-Governing Province Governor having jurisdiction over the location of the farmland subject to disposition (hereinafter referred to as the "Mayor/Do Governor") within 1 month from the date of the notice. <Added on Jun. 16, 2026>

Article 11 (Disposal orders and requests for purchase) (1) The head of a Si (referring to the head of a Si in which no Gu is established; hereafter in this Article the same shall apply)/Gun/Gu shall order any owner of farmland falling under any of the following subparagraphs to dispose of such farmland within 6 months: <Amended on Aug. 17, 2021; Jun. 16, 2026>
  1. Where the head of a Si/Gun/Gu deems that an owner of farmland owns the farmland after obtaining a qualification certificate for acquisition of farmland under Article 8(1) by fraud or other improper means;
  2. Where an owner of farmland fails to dispose of the farmland subject to disposal during the period of obligation for disposal under Article 10;
  3. Where the head of a Si/Gun/Gu deems that an agricultural corporation has engaged in the real estate business, in violation of Article 19-5 of the Act on Fostering and Supporting Agricultural and Fisheries Business Entities;
  4. Where a person who owns farmland within an area subject to permission of land transaction falls under any of the subparagraphs of Article 10(1).
  (2) Where an owner of farmland receives a disposal order under paragraph (1), he or she shall dispose of the farmland to a person who does not fall under any of the following subparagraphs; in such cases, the owner of farmland may request the Korea Rural Community Corporation to purchase the farmland: <Amended on Dec. 29, 2008; Jun. 16, 2026>
  1. In cases falling under paragraph (1)1, 3, or 4, a household member who shares the same household with the owner as of the date on which the ground giving rise thereto occurs;
  2. In cases falling under paragraph (1)2, a household member who shares the same household with the owner as of the date on which the ground causing the owner to fall under any subparagraph of Article 10(1) occurs;
  3. A spouse or a lineal ascendant or descendant;
  4. A corporation or organization represented by the owner;
  5. Where the owner is a corporation, a specially related person under Article 542-8(2)5 of the Commercial Act with respect to such corporation.
  (3) Upon receiving a request to purchase farmland under the latter part of paragraph (2), the Korea Rural Community Corporation shall purchase the relevant farmland within the budget of the Korea Rural Community Corporation, based on the officially assessed land price under the Act on the Public Announcement of Real Estate Values (referring to the individual land price calculated under Article 8 of that Act if there is no officially assessed land price for the relevant land; hereinafter the same shall apply), unless there is good cause prescribed by Presidential Decree. In such cases, if the actual transaction price in the neighboring area is lower than the officially assessed land price, the Corporation may purchase the farmland based on the actual transaction price. <Amended on Dec. 29, 2008; Jan. 19, 2016; Jun. 16, 2026>
  (4) Funds necessary for the Korea Rural Community Corporation to purchase farmland under paragraph (3) shall be loaned from the Farmland Management Fund under Article 31 of the Korea Rural Community Corporation and Farmland Management Fund Act (hereinafter referred to as the "Farmland Management Fund"). <Amended on Dec. 29, 2008; Jun. 16, 2026>
  (5) Where the head of a Si/Gun/Gu fails to issue a disposal order under paragraph (1) despite the occurrence of a ground by which an owner of farmland falls under any of the subparagraphs of paragraph (1), the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs or the Mayor/Do Governor may order the head of the Si/Gun/Gu to issue a disposal order under paragraph (1) within a specified period. <Added on Jun. 16, 2026>
  (6) If the head of a Si/Gun/Gu fails to issue a disposal order under paragraph (1) within the period under paragraph (5), the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs or the Mayor/Do Governor may directly issue a disposal order under paragraph (1) to the owner of farmland. <Added on Jun. 16, 2026>

Article 12 (Deferral of disposal orders) (1) Where an owner of farmland who has not disposed of his or her farmland subject to disposal within the period of obligation for disposal under Article 10(1) falls under any of the following cases, the head of a Si (referring to the head of a Si which has no Gu; hereinafter the same shall apply)/Gun/Gu may defer ex officio the disposal order under Article 11(1) for three years from the date the period of obligation for disposal has passed: <Amended on Dec. 29, 2008>
  1. Where he or she uses the relevant farmland for his or her own agricultural management;
  2. Where he or she has concluded a consignment sales contract for the relevant farmland with the Korea Rural Community Corporation or other persons prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  (2) Where an owner of farmland for whom a disposal order has been deferred under paragraph (1) ceases to fall under any of the subparagraphs of paragraph (1) during the period of deferral of the disposal order, the head of a Si/Gun/Gu shall order the disposal deferred without delay.
  (3) With regard to an owner of farmland subject to deferral of a disposal order, where the period of deferral has passed without him or her receiving a disposal order under paragraph (2), it shall be deemed that only the obligation for disposal of the farmland for which the disposal order was deferred has been extinguished with respect to the obligation for disposal under Article 10(1).
  (4) The head of a Si/Gun/Gu shall investigate annually whether an owner of farmland who has granted a deferral of a disposal order under paragraph (1) falls under any of the subparagraphs of paragraph (1) during the period of deferral of the disposal order, and shall report the results to the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs via the Mayor/Do Governor by March 31 of the following year. <Added on Jun. 16, 2026>
  (5) Based on the results reported under paragraph (4), where an owner of farmland does not fall under any of the subparagraphs of paragraph (1) during the period of deferral of the disposal order, the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs or the Mayor/Do Governor may order the head of a Si/Gun/Gu to issue the deferred disposal order within a specified period. <Added on Jun. 16, 2026>
  (6) If the head of a Si/Gun/Gu fails to issue the deferred disposal order within the period under paragraph (5), the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs or the Mayor/Do Governor may directly issue the deferred disposal order to the owner of farmland. <Added on Jun. 16, 2026>

Article 13 (Acquisition of mortgaged farmland) (1) If there is no successful bidder, even though auction to execute the mortgage right has been held on at least two occasions, a mortgagee of farmland who falls under any of the following subparagraphs may participate in auction for the mortgaged farmland held thereafter and acquire ownership of the mortgaged farmland: <Amended on Dec. 29, 2008; May 17, 2010; Mar. 31, 2011; May 19, 2011; May 29, 2016; Nov. 26, 2019>
  1. Regional agricultural cooperatives, regional livestock cooperatives, cooperatives by item or by business and their national federation and Nonghyup Bank under the Agricultural Cooperatives Act, district fisheries cooperatives, fisheries cooperatives by business, fisheries cooperatives for processed marine products, their national federation and Suhyup Bank under the Fisheries Cooperatives Act, regional forestry cooperatives, forestry cooperatives by item or by business and their national federation under the Forestry Cooperatives Act;
  2. The Korea Rural Community Corporation;
  3. Banks established pursuant to the Banking Act or other financial institutions prescribed by Presidential Decree;
  4. Korea Asset Management Corporation established under the Act on the Establishment of Korea Asset Management Corporation;
  5. A special purpose company, etc. defined in Article 3 of the Asset-Backed Securitization Act;
  6. The agricultural cooperative's asset management company established pursuant to the Act on the Structural Improvement of Agricultural Cooperatives.
  (2) The mortgagee of farmland referred to in paragraph (1)1 and 3 may entrust the disposition of the farmland that he or she has acquired pursuant to paragraph (1) to the Korea Rural Community Corporation. <Amended on Dec. 29, 2008>

CHAPTER III UTILIZATION OF FARMLAND

SECTION 1 Promotion of Utilization of Farmland

Article 14 Deleted. <Jan. 23, 2024>

Article 15 (Execution of farmland utilization promotion projects) A Mayor/Do Governor, the head of a Si/Gun/autonomous Gu, the Korea Rural Community Corporation, and any other person prescribed by Presidential Decree (hereinafter referred to as a "project implementer") may implement any of the following projects for the purpose of promoting the utilization of farmland (hereinafter referred to as "farmland utilization promotion projects"): <Amended on Dec. 29, 2008; Jan. 23, 2024; Feb. 27, 2026; Jun. 16, 2026>
  1. A project promoting the transfer of farmland ownership by means of sale and purchase, exchange, partition, merger, etc. of farmland;
  2. A project promoting establishment of the leasehold of farmland (including rights accruing from gratuitous loan for use; hereinafter the same shall apply) by means of the long-term lease or long-term gratuitous loan for use of farmland;
  3. A project promoting entrusted management;
  4. A project fostering agricultural management bodies in order to improve agricultural management by the joint use or collective use of farmland by farmers or agricultural corporations.

Article 16 (Requirements for farmland utilization promotion projects) A farmland utilization promotion project shall meet each of the following requirements:
  1. Farmland shall be used for the purpose of agricultural management;
  2. Establishment of the leasehold of, transfer of the ownership of farmland, entrustment of and being entrusted with agricultural management shall contribute to the expansion of the scale of the agricultural management of a farmer or an agricultural corporation or to the collectivization of farmland utilization;
  3. It shall contribute to promoting the efficiency of agricultural management by reducing expenses of agricultural management, including both production and distribution expenses of agricultural products through automation, mechanization, etc.

Article 17 (Formulation of implementation plans for farmland utilization promotion projects) (1) When a Mayor/Do Governor or the head of a Si/Gun/autonomous Gu intends to execute a farmland utilization promotion project, he or she shall establish an implementation plan for the farmland utilization promotion project, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs, and finalize the plan after undergoing deliberation by the deliberation council on policies for City/Do agriculture, rural communities, and the food industry (hereinafter referred to as the "deliberation council on policies for City/Do agriculture, rural communities, and the food industry") under Article 15 of the Framework Act on Agriculture, Rural Community and Food Industry or the deliberation council on policies for Si/Gun/Gu agriculture, fisheries, farming and fishing villages, and the food industry (hereinafter referred to as the "the deliberation council on policies for Si/Gun/Gu agriculture, rural communities, and the food industry"). The same shall also apply where he or she intends to amend an established plan. <Amended on Dec. 21, 2007; Feb. 29, 2008; May 27, 2009; Mar. 23, 2013; Jan. 23, 2024; Feb. 27, 2026>
  (2) When a project implementer other than a Mayor/Do Governor or the head of a Si/Gun/autonomous Gu intends to implement a farmland utilization promotion project, he or she shall establish an implementation plan for the farmland utilization promotion project, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs, and submit it to the head of a Si/Gun/autonomous Gu. <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008; Mar. 23, 2013; Feb. 27, 2026>
  (3) If deemed necessary to supplement an implementation plan for the farmland utilization promotion project submitted under paragraph (2), the head of a Si/Gun/autonomous Gu may request the relevant project implementer to supplement the plan, specifying the reasons and period for such supplementation.
  (4) Each of the following matters shall be included in an implementation plan for the farmland utilization promotion project: <Amended on Feb, 29, 2008; Mar. 23, 2013; Apr. 13, 2021>
  1. Areas where the farmland utilization promotion project is to be performed;
  2. Matters concerning a person who has ownership or a leasehold of farmland, a person for whom a leasehold will be established, a person to whom ownership will be transferred, or a person who entrusts agricultural management or who will be entrusted therewith;
  3. Matters concerning farmland for which the leasehold is established, farmland the ownership of which is transferred, farmland the agricultural management of which is entrusted, or farmland a person is entrusted with its agricultural management;
  4. Matters concerning the details of the leasehold to be established or the details of entrusting or being entrusted with agricultural management;
  5. The timing for transfer of the ownership, the price for the transfer, the payment method for price for the transfer, and other matters prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs.

Article 18 (Public notification and effect of implementation plans for farmland utilization promotion projects) (1) When a Mayor/Do Governor or the head of a Si/Gun/autonomous Gu has confirmed an implementation plan for the farmland utilization promotion project under Article 17(1) or has received such plan under paragraph (2) of the same Article (where he or she requested the project operator to supplement it under paragraph (3) of the same Article, the time the supplementation thereof has been completed), he or she shall, without delay, publicly notify the implementation plan, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs, and make it available for public perusal by related persons. <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008; Mar. 23, 2013; Feb. 27, 2026>
  (2) Where an implementation plan for the farmland utilization promotion project has been publicly notified under paragraph (1), the project implementer shall, as prescribed by Presidential Decree, entrust the registration of the relevant farmland, with the consent of the persons included in such plan and referred to in Article 17(4)2.
  (3) Where the project implementer entrusts the registration under paragraph (2), the papers which have finalized the implementation plan for the farmland utilization promotion project under Article 17(1) or the papers which have publicly notified the implementation plan for the farmland utilization promotion project under paragraph (1) and the written consent under paragraph (2) shall be deemed papers proving the cause of the registration under the Registration of Real Estate Act. <Amended on Apr. 12, 2011>
  (4) Article 3 of the Act on Special Measures for the Registration of Real Estate shall not apply to the entrustment of registration in accordance with an implementation plan for the farmland utilization promotion project.

Article 19 (Support for farmland utilization promotion projects) The State and local governments shall provide guidance and arrangement necessary for smoothly performing a farmland utilization promotion project and may partially subsidize expenses incurred in implementing such project within budgetary limits.

Article 20 (Designation of surrogate cultivators) (1) The head of a Si (referring to the head of a Si which has no Gus; hereafter the same shall apply in this Article)/Gun/Gu may ex officio designate a person who will cultivate idle farmland (referring to farmland prescribed by Presidential Decree as one not used for cultivating crops or growing perennial plants; hereinafter the same shall apply) instead of an owner or a lessee of that farmland (hereinafter referred to as "surrogate cultivator"), as prescribed by Presidential Decree, or receive applications from those who desire to cultivate idle farmland to designate surrogate cultivators, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs. <Amended on Jan. 17, 2012; Mar. 23, 2013>
  (2) When the head of a Si/Gun/Gu intends to designate a surrogate cultivator under paragraph (1), he or she shall give prior notice thereof to the owner or the lessee of the farmland, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs, and when he or she has designated a surrogate cultivator, he or she shall send a written notice of designation to the surrogate cultivator and the owner or the lessee of the farmland, respectively. <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008; Mar. 23, 2013>
  (3) The period for cultivation by surrogate cultivator shall be 3 years unless prescribed otherwise. <Amended on Jan. 17, 2012>
  (4) The surrogate cultivator shall pay 10/100 of the yield to the owner or the lessee of the relevant farmland as the rent of the farmland, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs. In such cases, if the owner or the lessee refuses to receive the rent or if it is impracticable to pay the rent, the surrogate cultivator may deposit the rent of the farmland. <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008; Mar. 23, 2013>
  (5) In order for the owner or the lessee of the farmland cultivated by surrogate cultivator to directly cultivate that farmland, he or she shall apply, no later than 3 months prior to the expiration of the period for cultivation by surrogate cultivator under paragraph (3) and after the expiration of the period for cultivation by surrogate cultivator, for the suspension of the designation of the surrogate cultivator to the head of a Si/Gun/Gu, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs, and upon receipt of such application, the head of a Si/Gun/Gu shall notify the surrogate cultivator and the owner or the lessee of the relevant farmland of the suspension of the designation of the surrogate cultivator within one month from the date of the receipt of the application. <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008; Mar. 23, 2013>
  (6) In any of the following cases, the head of a Si/Gun/Gu may terminate the designation of the surrogate cultivator even before the expiration of the period for cultivation by surrogate cultivator:
  1. Where the owner or the lessee of the farmland cultivated by a surrogate cultivator applies for termination of the designation as surrogate cultivator with good cause;
  2. Where the surrogate cultivator neglects his or her cultivation;
  3. Where there exist other reasons prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 21 (Improvement and preservation of soil) (1) The State and local governments shall implement projects to improve and preserve soil and devise policy measures concerning experiments, research, survey, etc. for improving and preserving soil so that farmers and agricultural corporations may continue environmental agricultural management.
  (2) In order to achieve the purposes referred to in paragraph (1), the State may partially subsidize, within budgetary limits, the expenses incurred by local governments, agricultural producers’ organizations, farmers, or agricultural corporations, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs in executing projects to improve and preserve soil. <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008; Mar. 23, 2013>

Article 22 (Prevention of fractionation of farmland ownership) (1) In order to prevent the fractionation of farmland ownership of farmers or agricultural corporations, the State and local governments may give necessary support so that farmland may be inherited by, donated, or transferred to, a farmer or an agricultural corporation en bloc.
  (2) No farmland where a rearrangement project for agricultural production infrastructure under the Agricultural and Fishing Villages Improvement Act has been performed shall be divided except under any of the following circumstances: <Amended on May 27, 2009; Apr. 14, 2011>
  1. Where the farmland included in a residential area, commercial area, or industrial area of an urban area, or in a site for urban/Gun planning facilities under the National Land Planning and Utilization Act is divided;
  2. Where the converted farmland is divided with permission for farmland conversion (including authorization, permission, approval, etc. which is legally construed as permission for farmland conversion under other statutes) under Article 34(1) or with the report on farmland conversion under Article 35 or 43;
  3. Where the land is divided so that the area of each parcel after the division exceeds 2,000 square meters;
  4. Where the farmland is divided due to reasons prescribed by Presidential Decree, including the improvement, exchange, division or merger, etc. of farmland.
  (3) The head of a Si/Gun/Gu may limit the maximum number of persons who intend to co-own one parcel of farmland (excluding cases under Article 6(2)4) to no more than seven persons, as prescribed by ordinance of the relevant Si/Gun/Gu, taking into consideration normal farming practices, etc., in order to efficiently utilize such farmland and enhance agricultural productivity. <Added on Aug. 17, 2021>

SECTION 2 Lease of Farmland

Article 23 (Lease or gratuitous loan for use of farmland) (1) No farmland shall be leased or allowed to be used free of charge, except in any of the following cases: <Amended on Dec. 29, 2008; May 27, 2009; Jan. 20, 2015; Jul. 20, 2015; Feb. 11, 2020; Aug. 16, 2023; Jun. 16, 2026>
  1. Where farmland falling under Article 6(2)1, 6 through 9, 9-2, and 10 is leased or allowed to be used free of charge;
  2. Where the farmland is leased or lent free of charge pursuant to an implementation plan for the farmland utilization promotion project under Article 17;
  3. Where the farmland owned is leased or lent free of charge by a person who has not be engaged temporarily in agricultural management on grounds of disease, conscription, school attendance, taking public office by election, or other inevitable reasons prescribed by Presidential Decree;
  4. Where the farmland that have been used for a person's own agricultural management for at least 5 years is leased or lent free of charge out of the pieces of the farmland owned by the person prescribed by Presidential Decree, who is aged 60 or older and has ceased to be engaged in agricultural management;
  5. Where the farmland owned by an individual under Article 6(1) for at least 3 years is leased or lent free of charge to a person who intends to conduct weekend or experiential farming, or to a person who engages in the business of leasing farmland to a person intending to conduct weekend or experiential farming;
  5-2. Where the farmland owned by an agricultural corporation pursuant to Article 6(1) is leased or lent free of charge to a person who intends to conduct weekend or experiential farming;
  6. Where the farmland owned by an individual pursuant to Article 6(1) for at least 3 years is leased or lent free of charge upon entrustment to the Korea Rural Community Corporation or other persons prescribed by Presidential Decree;
  7. Where any of the following farmland is leased or lent free of charge upon entrustment to the Korea Rural Community Corporation and other persons prescribed by Presidential Decree:
  a. Farmland owned by a person who acquired the farmland by inheritance and does not engage in agricultural management;
  b. Farmland owned by a person who has left farming after having engaged in agricultural management for at least the period prescribed by Presidential Decree;
  8. Where farmland for self-cultivation is leased or lent free of charge for up to 8 months for double cropping prescribed by the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs;
  9. Where farmland for self-cultivation is leased or lent free of charge as such farmland is necessary to promote business conducted for the purpose of scale-up of farmland prescribed by Presidential Decree, stability of demand for and supply of crops, etc.
  (2) Notwithstanding paragraph (1), where a lessee or a borrower under a gratuitous loan for use fails to use the farmland for his or her own agricultural management without good cause, the head of a Si/Gun/Gu may order the termination of the lease or gratuitous loan for use, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs. <Added on Jul. 20, 2015>
  (3) Where farmland is leased or allowed to be used free of charge pursuant to paragraph (1)6 or 7, matters prescribed by Presidential Decree, such as the selection of lessees, shall be delegated to the person to whom the farmland is entrusted. <Added on Jun. 16, 2026>

Article 24 (Contracting method for lease or gratuitous loan for use of farmland and confirmation thereof) (1) A lease agreement (applicable only to cases of leasing to a person who intends to engage in agricultural management; hereinafter the same shall apply in this Section) and a gratuitous-loan-for-use agreement (applicable only to cases of allowing a person who intends to engage in agricultural management to use farmland free of charge) shall, in principle, be concluded in writing. <Amended on Feb. 11, 2020>
  (2) Each lease agreement referred to in paragraph (1) shall, despite the non-existence of its registration, begin to take effect against a third party from the date following the date on which the lessee obtains confirmation from the head of a Si/Gu/Eup/Myeon having jurisdiction over the location of the farmland and takes delivery of the farmland.
  (3) The head of a Si/Gu/Eup/Myeon shall maintain a farmland lease agreement confirmation register, and where a lessor or lessee holding a written lease agreement applies for confirmation, he or she shall, after confirming the lease agreement as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs, record the details thereof in the register. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Jan. 17, 2012]

Article 24-2 (Term of lease) (1) The term of lease of farmland under the subparagraphs of Article 23(1) (excluding subparagraph 8) shall be at least 3 years; provided, the term of lease of farmland prescribed by Presidential Decree, such as land for cultivating perennial plants, shall be at least 5 years. <Amended on Jan. 20, 2015; Jul. 20, 2015; Feb. 11, 2020>
  (2) Where the term of lease is not set or is shorter than the period prescribed in paragraph (1), the lease shall be deemed agreed for the period prescribed in paragraph (1); provided, a lessee may claim that the term of lease shorter than the period under paragraph (1) remain valid. <Amended on Feb. 11, 2020>
  (3) Notwithstanding paragraphs (1) and (2), a lessor may set the term of lease to be shorter than the period prescribed in paragraph (1) when there is any unavoidable reason prescribed by Presidential Decree, such as diseases and conscription. <Amended on Feb. 11, 2020>
  (4) The term of lease under paragraphs (1) through (3) shall also equally apply where the lease agreement is extended, renewed or re-concluded. <Amended on Feb. 11, 2020>
  [This Article Added on Jan. 17, 2012]

Article 24-3 (Mediation concerning lease agreements) (1) When the contracting parties to a lease agreement fails to reach a mutual agreement on the lease agreement in terms of the term of lease, rents, etc., they may apply for mediation to the head of a Si/Gun/autonomous Gu having jurisdiction over the location of the farmland.
  (2) The head of a Si/Gun/autonomous Gu shall, upon receipt of an application for mediation pursuant to paragraph (1), without delay organize a farmland lease mediation committee and commence the mediation procedures.
  (3) When the contracting parties to a lease agreement accept a mediation proposal prepared by the farmland lease mediation committee referred to in paragraph (2), such mediation proposal shall be construed as the terms and conditions of the contract concluded between the contracting parties to the relevant lease.
  (4) The farmland lease mediation committee referred to in paragraph (2) shall be comprised of 3 members, including 1 chairperson, and the chairperson shall be the deputy head of a Si/Gun/autonomous Gu, and the members thereof shall be commissioned by the head of a Si/Gun/autonomous Gu, from among those who have nothing to do with those stakeholders in the mediation and who are the members of the deliberation council on policies for Si/Gun/Gu agriculture, rural communities, and the food industry. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Jun. 22, 2015; Jan. 23, 2024>
  (5) Matters necessary for the organization, operation, etc. of farmland lease mediation committees referred to in paragraph (2) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  [This Article Added on Jan. 17, 2012]

Article 25 (Implicit renewal) Where the lessor fails to provide the lessee with a notice of his or her intention that he or she will not renew the lease agreement or will change the terms and conditions of the lease agreement no later than 3 months before the lease ends, the lease shall be deemed renewed under the same terms and conditions as the previous one at the time the validity of the lease has been terminated. <Amended on Jan. 17, 2012>
  [Title Amended on Jan. 17, 2012]

Article 26 (Succession to legal status of lessor) A transferee of the leased farmland shall be deemed succeeded to the legal status of the lessor of the farmland under this Act.

Article 26-2 (Imperative provisions) Any contract in contravention of this Act and to the disadvantage of the lessee shall be nullified.
  [This Article Added on Jan. 17, 2012]

Article 27 (Special cases concerning lease of state farmland and public farmland) The provisions of Articles 24, 24-2, 24-3, 25, 26, and 26-2 shall not apply to the farmland which constitutes the State property or the public property under the State Property Act or the Public Property and Commodity Management Act. <Amended on Jan. 17, 2012>

CHAPTER IV PRESERVATION OF FARMLAND

SECTION 1 Designation and Use of Agricultural Promotion Areas

Article 28 (Designation of agricultural promotion areas) (1) Mayors/Do Governors shall designate an agricultural promotion area for the efficient utilization and preservation of farmland. <Amended on Jan. 23, 2024; Feb. 27, 2026>
  (2) Agricultural promotion areas referred to in paragraph (1) may be designated as classified into the following specific-use areas: <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008; Mar. 23, 2013>
  1. Agricultural promotion areas: either of the following areas for which agricultural promotion is needed be planned and which are required to be used for agricultural purposes through the collectivization of farmland to the scale prescribed by the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs:
  a. An area where land being used or to be used for agricultural purposes has been collectivized and a farmland creation project or an agricultural infrastructure rearrangement project has been performed or is being performed;
  b. An area, other than that falling under item a, where land being used for agricultural purposes has been collectivized;
  2. Agricultural protection areas: an area necessary for protecting agricultural environment, including securing sources of water supply and preserving water quality in an agricultural promotion area.

Article 29 (Areas eligible for designation as agricultural promotion areas) Greenbelts, management areas, agriculture and forestry areas, and natural environment preservation areas under the National Land Planning and Utilization Act are eligible for designation as an agricultural promotion area under Article 28; provided, green-belts in the Special Metropolitan City shall be excluded.

Article 30 (Procedures for designation of agricultural promotion areas) (1) A Mayor/Do Governor shall designate agricultural promotion areas after deliberation by the deliberation council on policies for City/Do agriculture, rural communities, and the food industry and upon obtaining approval from the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs. <Amended on Dec. 21, 2007; Feb. 29, 2008; May 27, 2009; Mar. 23, 2013; Jun. 22, 2015; Feb. 27, 2026>
  (2) Where a Mayor/Do Governor designates an agricultural promotion area under paragraph (1), he or she shall, without delay, publicly notify such fact and notify the relevant agencies thereof, and shall have the head of a Si/Gun/autonomous Gu make the relevant information available for public inspection.
  (3) Where a greenbelt or planned control area designated pursuant to the National Land Planning and Utilization Act is included in an agricultural promotion area, the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs shall consult with the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport before he or she approves the designation of the agricultural promotion area under paragraph (1). <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008; Mar. 23, 2013>
  (4) Procedures for designation of agricultural promotion areas and other necessary matters for such designation shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 31 (Change and cancellation of agricultural promotion areas) (1) Where there exist grounds prescribed by Presidential Decree, a Mayor/Do Governor may change or cancel an agricultural promotion area or a specific-use area; provided, where such grounds do not exist, the agricultural promotion area or specific-use area shall revert to the original one. <Amended on Dec. 24, 2018>
  (2) Article 30 shall apply mutatis mutandis to the procedures, etc. for change, cancellation, or reversion of agricultural promotion areas or specific-use areas prescribed in paragraph (1); provided, any change in matters prescribed by Presidential Decree, including reversion to the original agricultural promotion area or specific-use area prescribed in the proviso of paragraph (1) or change of an agricultural protection area into an agricultural promotion area, may be made without deliberation by the deliberation council on policies for City/Do agriculture, rural communities, and the food industry or the approval of the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs, as prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on Dec. 21, 2007; Feb. 29, 2008; May 27, 2009; Mar. 23, 2013; Dec. 24, 2018>

Article 31-2 (Listening to opinions of residents) When a Mayor/Do Governor intends to designate, change, or cancel an agricultural promotion area pursuant to Articles 30 and 31, he or she shall give prior notice of the details thereof to each owner of the relevant land and listen to the opinions of the relevant local residents, as prescribed by Presidential Decree; provided, the same shall not apply to either of the following circumstances:
  1. When notice is given to each of the land owners pursuant to other statutes;
  2. When any person to whom notice shall be given remains unknown or his or her address, residence or place where notice shall be given remains unknown.
  [This Article Added on Jan. 17, 2012]

Article 31-3 (Fact-finding surveys) (1) The Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs shall conduct the following surveys every year to efficiently manage farmland: <Amended on Aug. 17, 2021>
  1. A survey on idle farmland under Article 20(1);
  2. A fact-finding survey on agricultural promotion areas under Article 28;
  3. A survey on the current status of farmland registered in the information system under Article 54-2(3);
  4. Other surveys on the matters prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs.
  (2) Where the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs deems that any ground for changing or canceling an agricultural promotion area, etc. prescribed in Article 31(1) occurs based on the results of a fact-finding survey on agricultural promotion areas conducted under paragraph (1)2, the Mayor/Do Governor may change or cancel the relevant agricultural promotion area or specific-use area. <Amended on Aug. 17, 2021>
  (3) Other matters necessary for the scope, method, etc. of a fact-finding survey under paragraph (1) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  [This Article Added on Dec. 24, 2018]

Article 32 (Restrictions on Acts in specific-use areas) (1) No one shall engage in any land use activities, other than those prescribed by Presidential Decree and directly related to agricultural production or farmland improvement, within an agricultural promotion area; provided, this shall not apply to the following land use activities: <Amended on May 27, 2009; Jan. 17, 2012; Dec. 24, 2018; Feb. 11, 2020; May 16, 2023; Aug. 8, 2023; Jun. 16, 2026>
  1. Installation of processing and treatment facilities for agricultural and fishery products prescribed by Presidential Decree (referring to agricultural, forest, stock farm, and fishery products; hereinafter the same shall apply) and of experimental and research facilities related to the agricultural and fisheries industries (referring to the agricultural, forestry, livestock, and fisheries industries; hereinafter the same shall apply);
  2. Installation of children's playgrounds, village halls, and other convenience facilities and welfare facilities necessary for the community life of farmers or rural residents, as prescribed by Presidential Decree;
  3. Installation of farmers' housing, fishers' housing, agricultural facilities, livestock facilities, or fishery facilities prescribed by Presidential Decree;
  4. Installation of national defense and military facilities;
  5. Creation of rivers, installation of banks or other facilities corresponding thereto for the preservation of national land;
  6. Repairs, restoration, and movement of national heritage defined in Article 3 of the Framework Act on National Heritage, excavation of buried heritage, and installation of tombstones, monuments, or other similar structures;
  7. Installation of roads, railroads, and other public facilities prescribed by Presidential Decree;
  8. Using land as a place for exploration for the exploitation of underground resources or for mining of underground minerals and for concentration and piling up of ores;
  9. Installation of facilities prescribed by Presidential Decree and necessary for development of agricultural and fishing villages, including the development of income sources of agricultural and fishing villages.
  (2) In an agricultural protection area, no land use activity other than those specified in the following subparagraphs shall be conducted: <Amended on Feb. 11, 2020>
  1. Land use activities permitted under paragraph (1);
  2. Installation of buildings, structures, and other facilities prescribed by Presidential Decree and necessary for the increase of farmers’ incomes;
  3. Installation of buildings, structures, and other facilities prescribed by Presidential Decree and necessary for improving the living conditions of farmers.
  (3) The provisions of paragraphs (1) and (2) shall not apply to existing buildings, structures, and other facilities installed with the authorization, permission, approval, etc. obtained or with the report made under the relevant statutes or regulations as at the time when the agricultural promotion area is designated.
  (4) The provisions of paragraphs (1) and (2) shall not apply to any construction works or project being performed by the person (referring to the person who has commenced the construction works or the project in progress where it is not required to obtain authorization, permission, approval, etc. or make a report under the relevant statutes or regulations) who is performing the construction work or the project after obtaining authorization, permission, approval, etc. for, or making a report on, the following acts under the relevant statutes or regulations as at the time when the agricultural promotion area is designated:
  1. Construction of buildings;
  2. Installation of structures and other facilities;
  3. Changes in the form and quality of the land;
  4. Other acts corresponding to those referred to in subparagraphs 1 through 3.

Article 33 (Expanding development of, and investment in, agricultural promotion areas and giving priority to supporting such areas) (1) The State and local governments shall give priority in making investment in the improvement and rearrangement of farmland and agricultural facilities, the expansion of road for agricultural and fishing villages and of distribution facilities for agricultural products, and other projects for agricultural development in an agricultural promotion area, as prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  (2) The State and local governments shall give priority in providing necessary support, including offering subsidies or reducing tax under the Restriction of Special Taxation Act, to farmers or agricultural corporations cultivating crops or growing perennial plants on farmland in an agricultural promotion area.

Article 33-2 (Requests for purchase of farmland in agricultural promotion areas) (1) A farmer or an agricultural corporation owning farmland in an agricultural promotion area may request the Korea Rural Community Corporation to purchase such farmland. <Amended on Jun. 16, 2026>
  (2) Upon receipt of a request for purchase made under paragraph (1), the Korea Rural Community Corporation may purchase the relevant farmland based on the value appraised by appraisal corporations, etc. provided in the Act on Appraisal and Certified Appraisers. <Amended on Jan. 19, 2016; Apr. 7, 2020>
  (3) Expenses incurred by the Korea Rural Community Corporation in purchasing farmland pursuant to paragraph (2) shall be loaned from the Farmland Management Fund.
  [This Article Added on Jan. 17, 2012]

SECTION 2 Farmland Conversion

Article 34 (Permission to convert farmland and consultation) (1) Any person who intends to convert farmland shall obtain permission from the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs (including consultation deemed permission for farmland conversion under other statutes; hereinafter the same shall apply), as prescribed by Presidential Decree, except in any of the following cases. The same shall also apply where he or she intends to change significant matters prescribed by Presidential Decree, including the area or boundary of the permitted farmland: <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008; May 27, 2009; Mar. 23, 2013; Aug. 16, 2023>
  1. Deleted. <Aug. 16, 2023>
  2. Where the conversion is made to the farmland following consultation made under paragraph (2) or the farmland excluded from that subject to consultation under the proviso of paragraph (2)1 as farmland in an urban area or planned control area under the National Land Planning and Utilization Act;
  3. Where the conversion of the farmland is made upon filing a report on farmland conversion under Article 35;
  4. Where the farmland illegally reclaimed without obtaining permission to convert a mountainous district under Article 14 of the Mountainous Districts Management Act or without filing a report on conversion of a mountainous district under Article 15 of the same Act is restored to a forest;
  5. Deleted. <Jan. 2, 2024>
  (2) In any of the following cases, the competent Minister or the head of a local government shall consult in advance on the farmland conversion with the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs, as prescribed by Presidential Decree: <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008; May 27, 2009; Apr. 14, 2011; Mar. 23, 2013; Jan. 2, 2024>
  1. Where farmland is included in the proposed site to be designated as a residential area, commercial area, or industrial area or in the proposed site for urban/Gun planning facilities at the time of designating such area in an urban area under the National Land Planning and Utilization Act or determining such facilities in an urban area under that Act; provided, this shall not apply where an already designated residential area, commercial area, or industrial area is changed to an area for another purpose or where urban/Gun planning facilities are determined to be installed in an already designated residential area, commercial area or industrial area;
  1-2. When designating a district unit planning zone in a planned control area under the National Land Planning and Utilization Act, where farmland is included in the relevant proposed site;
  2. Where any development activity is permitted under Article 56 of the National Land Planning and Utilization Act on farmland in a green-belt or a development restriction zone in an urban area under that Act, or changes in the form and quality of the land is permitted under the proviso, with the exception of the subparagraphs, of Article 12(1) of the Act on Special Measures for Designation and Management of Development Restriction Zones.

Article 35 (Report on farmland conversion) (1) Any person who intends to convert farmland to a site for any of the following facilities shall file a report thereon with the head of a Si/Gun/autonomous Gu, as prescribed by Presidential Decree. The same shall also apply to any modification to the reported matters: <Amended on May 27, 2009; Jan. 17, 2012>
  1. Farmers' housing, fishers' housing, facilities for agriculture and livestock (excluding improvement facilities and production facilities for agricultural and stock farm products under subparagraph 1b of Article 2), and facilities for distribution and processing of agricultural and fishery products;
  2. Convenience facilities for community life of farmers, including children's playground and village hall;
  3. Research facilities related to agriculture and fisheries, and fisheries facilities, including fish farms and fish nurseries.
  (2) Where the head of a Si/Gun/autonomous Gu receives a report prescribed in paragraph (1), he or she shall review the details thereof, and shall accept the report if they are in compliance with this Act. <Added on Feb. 11, 2020>
  (3) Matters concerning the scope and scale of the facilities subject to reporting, restrictions on installation in an agricultural promotion area, the scope of the installers, etc. under paragraph (1) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on Feb. 11, 2020>

Article 36 (Permission for temporarily use of farmland for other purposes) (1) Any person who intends to temporarily use farmland for any of the following purposes shall obtain permission from the head of a Si/Gun/autonomous Gu on condition that he or she will restore it to farmland after having used it for such other purpose for a certain period, as prescribed by Presidential Decree. The same shall also apply to any modification to the permitted matters; provided, the State or a local government shall consult with the head of a Si/Gun/autonomous Gu: <Amended on Dec. 24, 2018; Jan. 2, 2024; Mar. 17, 2026; Jun. 16, 2026>
  1. Where installing simple facilities for agriculture, fisheries, and livestock (excluding improvement facilities and production facilities for agricultural and stock farm products prescribed in subparagraph 1b of Article 2) and simple facilities for treatment of agricultural and fishery products which are not subject to a building permit or building report prescribed in the Building Act;
  2. Where installing a field office or incidental facilities, other facilities corresponding thereto or piling up or burying goods for a principally intended project (applicable only to any project permitted in relevant farmland);
  3. Where excavating soil, stones, and minerals prescribed by Presidential Decree;
  4. Where power plants for solar energy referred to in subparagraph 2a of Article 2 of the Act on the Promotion of the Development, Use and Diffusion of Renewable Energy (hereinafter referred to as "solar energy power plant") to be installed for the purpose of operating electric utility business prescribed in subparagraph 1 of Article 2 of the Electric Utility Act shall meet all of the following requirements:
  a. Facilities shall be installed in an area prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs, such as an area which contains at least a certain level of salt in the soil among land formed by the reclamation of public waters prescribed in Article 2 of the Public Waters Management and Reclamation Act;
  b. Facilities shall be installed to meet the requirements specifically determined by the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs, such as the scale of installment and the method for measuring the salinity.
  4-2. Where agrivoltaic power generation facilities under subparagraph 5 of Article 2 of the Act on the Promotion and Support of Agrivoltaic Power Generation Business (hereinafter referred to as "agrivoltaic power generation facilities") are installed on farmland outside an agricultural promotion area or on other farmland prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs for the purpose of conducting an agrivoltaic power generation business under subparagraph 6 of Article 2 of that Act;
  5. Where installing facilities meeting all of the requirements prescribed by Presidential Decree, which adopt information and communications technology for improving agricultural productivity, among crop cultivating facilities that are not subject to building permission or building report under the Building Act (excluding fixed temperature rooms, mushroom cultivating facilities, and plastic greenhouses);
  6. Where installing facilities meeting all of the requirements prescribed by Presidential Decree as agricultural, fishing, and mountain village experience facilities that are not subject to building permission or building report under the Building Act;
  (2) Where the competent Minister or the head of a local government requests consultation regarding the temporary use of farmland for another purpose in connection with authorization, permission, approval, etc. of a project or project plan under another statute, the head of a Si/Gun/autonomous Gu may conduct such consultation on the condition that, when granting such authorization, permission, approval, etc., the project implementer will be required to restore the farmland after using it for a specified period; provided, where solar energy generation facilities are to be installed, such consultation shall be limited to cases falling under paragraph (1)4, and where agrivoltaic power generation facilities are to be installed, such consultation shall be limited to cases falling under paragraph (1)4-2. <Amended on Jun. 16, 2026>
  (3) Where the head of a Si/Gun/autonomous Gu grants permission prescribed in paragraph (1) or holds consultation prescribed in paragraph (2), he or she may have a person who intends to implement the project submit the plan for restoring the farmland to the original state and deposit restoration costs, as prescribed by Presidential Decree. In such cases, where the project implementer fails to implement the plan for restoring the farmland to the original state after such project was completed, the restoration costs deposited may be used as expenses incurred in restoring by proxy. <Amended on Dec. 24, 2018>
  (4) Where a person failed to complete the intended project after the temporary use of farmland for other purposes and thus intends to extend the period for such project pursuant to paragraph (1) and (2), the head of a Si/Gun/autonomous Gu shall recalculate restoration costs, as prescribed by Presidential Decree, and have him or her additionally deposit the difference if the restoration costs deposited under paragraph (3) are less than those recalculated. <Added on Dec. 24, 2018>
  (5) Standards for the calculation of, and timing and procedure for the payment of, restoration costs, and other necessary matters prescribed in paragraphs (3) and (4) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on Dec. 24, 2018>

Article 36-2 (Reports on temporary use of farmland for other purposes) (1) A person who intends to temporarily use farmland for any of the following purposes shall report thereon to the head of a Si/Gun/autonomous Gu, as prescribed by Presidential Decree, on the condition that he or she will restore the farmland to its original state after using it for a certain period without undermining its soil fertility. The same shall also apply where any change is made to the reported matters; provided, in cases of the State and a local government, it shall consult with the head of a Si/Gun/autonomous Gu:
  1. Where the person temporarily uses farmland as sledding slopes, places of local festivals, etc.;
  2. Where the person temporarily installs a facility falling under Article 36(1)1 or 2.
  (2) Where the competent Minister or the head of a local government requests consultations on the temporary use of farmland for other purposes in relation to the authorization, permission, approval, etc. for a project or project plan prescribed in other statutes, the head of a Si/Gun/autonomous Gu may consult with the competent Minister or the head of a local government, on the premise that the competent Minister or the head of a local government, when granting such authorization, permission, approval, etc., shall impose on a person who intends to implement the relevant projects conditions that the person will restore farmland to its original state after using it for a certain period.
  (3) When the head of a Si/Gun/autonomous Gu accepts a report prescribed in paragraph (1) or holds consultation prescribed in paragraph (2), he or she may require a person who intends to implement the project to submit a plan for restoring farmland to its original state and to deposit restoration costs, as prescribed by Presidential Decree. In such cases, where the project implementer fails to implement the plan for restoring the farmland to the original state after such project was completed, restoration costs deposited may be used as expenses incurred in restoring by proxy. <Amended on Dec. 24, 2018>
  (4) The head of a Si/Gun/autonomous Gu shall notify a person who has made a report of whether to accept the report, within 10 days from the date he or she receives the report prescribed in paragraph (1).
  (5) Where the head of a Si/Gun/autonomous Gu fails to notify a person who has made a report of whether to accept such report or of the extension of the processing period under statutes or regulations related to civil petition services within the period prescribed in paragraph (4), the report shall be deemed to be accepted on the day following the day on which the relevant period ends. (where the processing period is extended or re-extended under statutes or regulations related to civil petition services, it refers to the relevant processing period.)
  (6) The scope and scale of farmland to be reported under paragraph (1), period of temporary use, criteria for calculating restoration costs prescribed in paragraph (3), deadline and procedures for paying restoration costs, and other necessary matters shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  [This Article Added on Oct. 31, 2017]

Article 37 (Restrictions on permission for farmland conversion) (1) Where the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs decides to grant permission for farmland conversion under Article 34(1), he or she shall not permit the farmland conversion to be used as a site for any of the following facilities; provided, he or she may permit the farmland conversion in an urban area, planned control area, or development promotion area under the National Land Planning and Utilization Act, even if it is used as a site for any of the following facilities: <Amended on May 17, 2007; Feb. 29, 2008; Mar. 23, 2013; Jan. 17, 2017; Jan. 2, 2024>
  1. Air pollutants discharge facilities defined in subparagraph 11 of Article 2 of the Clean Air Conservation Act, as prescribed by Presidential Decree;
  2. Wastewater discharge facilities defined in subparagraph 10 of Article 2 of the Water Environment Conservation Act, as prescribed by Presidential Decree;
  3. Facilities prescribed by Presidential Decree, which are likely to impair agricultural promotion or the preservation of farmland.
  (2) When the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs, or the head of a Si/Gun/autonomous Gu grants permission for farmland conversion or has consultation on the farmland conversion under Article 34(1) or (2) or grants permission for and has consultation on any temporary use of farmland for another purpose under Article 36, he or she may restrict the farmland conversion or the temporary use of farmland for other purposes where such farmland falls under any of the following subparagraphs: <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008; Mar. 23, 2013; Aug. 16, 2023>
  1. Where the farmland to be converted needs to be preserved as superior farmland because it is included in the area where agricultural production infrastructure has been rearranged or where a rearrangement project for agricultural production infrastructure is to be performed;
  2. Where the conversion of the relevant farmland or the temporary use of such farmland for another purpose seriously hinders sunshine, ventilation, and consolidated cultivation or brings about the removal of farmland improvement facilities, thereby substantially affecting the agricultural management of nearby farmland;
  3. Where the conversion of the relevant farmland or the temporary use of such farmland for another purpose is likely to impair nearby farmland or farmland improvement facilities, including soil erosion;
  4. Where the project plan and the funding plan to realize the objectives of the conversion are unclear;
  5. Where the area of the farmland to be converted is excessively larger than that needed to realize the objectives of the conversion.

Article 37-2 (Application standards when granting permission for farmland conversion which extends over at least two special-purpose areas or special-purpose districts) Where a parcel of farmland extends over urban areas, planned control areas, development promotion districts, and special-purpose areas or special-purpose districts (referring to the special-purpose areas or special-purpose districts under Article 36(1) or 37(1) of the National Land Planning and Utilization Act; hereafter in this Article, the same shall apply) under the National Land Planning and Utilization Act and where the area of a special-purpose area or special-purpose district that occupies the smallest part of such farmland is not more than the area prescribed by Presidential Decree, Article 37(1) shall apply based on a special-purpose area or special-purpose district that occupies the largest part of the farmland.
  [This Article Added on Feb. 11, 2020]

Article 37-3 (Establishment and operation of Farmland Management Committee) (1) A Farmland Management Committee (hereinafter referred to as the "Committee") shall be established in the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs to provide advice on the following:
  1. Matters regarding the formulation of policies on the use, conservation, etc. of farmland;
  2. Matters regarding the farmland conversion on at least the scale prescribed by Presidential Decree among the matters requiring permission for farmland conversion or consultation on the farmland conversion under Article 34 or reports on the farmland conversion under Article 35;
  3. Other matters the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs deems necessary and refers to the Committee for deliberation.
  (2) The Committee shall be composed of up to 20 members including 1 chairperson.
  (3) Members of the Committee shall be commissioned by the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs from among public officials of relevant administrative agencies and persons with extensive knowledge of and experience in fields related to agriculture, rural communities, land use, spatial information, the environment, etc., and the chairperson shall be elected among and by members.
  (4) The term of office of the chairperson and each member shall be 2 years.
  (5) Matters necessary for the composition, operation, etc. of the Committee shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  [This Article Added on Aug. 17, 2021]

Article 38 (Farmland preservation charges) (1) Any of the following persons shall pay charges for preservation, management, and creation of farmland (hereinafter referred to as "farmland preservation charges") to a person who operates and manages the Farmland Management Fund: <Amended on May 27, 2009>
  1. A person who has obtained permission for farmland conversion pursuant to Article 34(1);
  2. A person who intends to convert farmland (including farmland excluded from consultation under the proviso of Article 34(2)1) in a proposed site for an area or facilities, for which consultation on the farmland conversion has been completed, under Article 34(2)1;
  2-2. A person who intends to convert farmland in a proposed site for an area, for which consultation on the farmland conversion has been completed under Article 34(2)1-2;
  3. A person who intends to convert farmland, for which consultation on the farmland conversion has been completed under Article 34(2)2;
  4. Deleted. <Aug. 16, 2023>
  5. A person who intends to convert farmland after filing a report on farmland conversion pursuant to Article 35 or 43.
  (2) Where the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs deems that it is impracticable to pay farmland preservation charges in a lump-sum for any of the following grounds, he or she may allow any person to pay farmland preservation charges in installments, as prescribed by Presidential Decree: <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008; Mar. 23, 2013; Jan. 20, 2015>
  1. Farmland conversion prescribed by Presidential Decree, such as cases where a public institution prescribed in the Act on the Management of Public Institutions or a local public enterprise prescribed in the Local Public Enterprises Act converts farmland to land for facilities in an industrial complex;
  2. Where farmland preservation charges are more than the amount of money prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs.
  (3) Where the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs intends to allow the installment payments of farmland preservation charges prescribed in paragraph (2), he or she shall have a person who intends to pay the farmland preservation charges in installments deposit in advance the insurance certificate of payment guarantee, etc. for the farmland preservation charges to be paid in installments, as prescribed by Presidential Decree; provided, the same shall not apply where a person who intends to pay the farmland preservation charges in installments is the State, a local government, or any other person prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008; Mar. 23, 2013>
  (4) Any person who intends to convert farmland shall pay all or part of farmland preservation charges prescribed in paragraph (1) or (2) before obtaining permission for farmland conversion or making a report on farmland conversion (including authorization, permission, approval, etc. which is legally construed as permission for farmland conversion or the reporting on the farmland conversion under other statutes). <Added on Jan. 20, 2015>
  (5) In any of the following cases, any person who operates and manages the Farmland Management Fund shall refund farmland preservation charges equivalent thereto, as prescribed by Presidential Decree: <Amended on Jan. 20, 2015>
  1. Where permission granted to a person who has paid farmland preservation charges is revoked pursuant to Article 39;
  2. Where a project plan of a person who has paid farmland preservation charges is altered;
  2-2. Where a person has paid farmland preservation charges pursuant to paragraph (4) but has failed to obtain permission;
  3. Where the area of farmland intended to be converted for grounds corresponding thereto has been reduced, compared with the original area thereof.
  (6) In any of the following cases, the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs may reduce or exempt the farmland preservation charges, as prescribed by Presidential Decree: <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008; Mar. 23, 2013; Jan. 20, 2015>
  1. Where the State or a local government converts farmland for official or public purposes;
  2. Where farmland is converted to install important industrial facilities prescribed by Presidential Decree;
  3. Where farmland is converted to install facilities referred to in any subparagraph of Article 35(1) or other facilities prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  (7) The amount of farmland preservation charges shall be computed by applying criteria for imposition prescribed by Presidential Decree within the officially assessed individual land price of the relevant farmland under the Act on the Public Announcement of Real Estate Values, but the criteria for imposition may be differently applied to farmland in and out of agricultural promotion areas and the date of applying the criteria for imposition shall be as follows: <Amended on Jan. 20, 2015; Jan. 19, 2016; Dec. 24, 2018>
  1. Where a person obtains permission for farmland conversion pursuant to Article 34(1): The date of applying for permission;
  2. Where a person intends to convert farmland pursuant to Article 34(2): The date prescribed by Presidential Decree;
  3. Where a person intends to convert farmland for which consultation legally construed as permission for farmland conversion has been completed under other statutes: The date prescribed by Presidential Decree;
  4. Where a person intends to convert farmland after filing a report on farmland conversion prescribed in Article 35 or 43: The date of receiving the report.
  (8) Where a person liable to pay the farmland preservation charges fails to pay them by the deadline for payment, the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs shall issue a demand notice stating the deadline not exceeding 30 days from the deadline for payment, within 10 days after the deadline for payment expires. <Amended on May 27, 2009; Mar. 23, 2013; Jan. 20, 2015; Dec. 24, 2018>
  (9) Where a person liable to pay the farmland preservation charges fails to pay them by the deadline for payment, the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs shall impose an amount of money equivalent to 3/100 of the farmland preservation charges in arrears on him or her as an additional charge from the date on which the deadline for payment expires: <Added on May 27, 2009; Jan. 17, 2012; Mar. 23, 2013; Jan. 20, 2015; Dec. 24, 2018>
  1. Deleted. <Jan. 20, 2015>
  2. Deleted. <Jan. 20, 2015>
  (10) Where a person who fell into arrears with farmland preservation charges fails to pay them, the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs shall impose an additional charge (hereinafter referred to as "aggravated additional charge") equivalent to 12/1000 of farmland preservation charges in arrears in addition to an additional charge prescribed in paragraph (9) whenever one month elapses from the date on which the deadline for payment expires. Where the amount of farmland preservation charges in arrears is less than 1 million won, however, he or she shall not impose an aggravated additional charge. In such cases, the period during which an aggravated additional charge is collected in addition to an additional charge shall not exceed 60 months. <Added on Jan. 20, 2015>
  (11) Where a person liable to pay farmland preservation charges fails to pay such charges, an additional charge and aggravated additional charge by the designated deadline for payment after he or she has received a demand notice, the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs may collect the relevant charges in the same manner as delinquent national or local taxes are collected. <Added on May 27, 2009; Mar. 23, 2013; Jan. 20, 2015>
  (12) Where any of the following causes occurs, the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs may write off the relevant farmland preservation charges; provided, where he or she discovers property he or she may seize after he or she has written off the farmland preservation charges in cases falling under subparagraphs 1, 3 and 4, he or she shall, without delay, revoke the write-off and take disposition on delinquency: <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008; May 27, 2009; Mar. 23, 2013; Jan. 20, 2015>
  1. Where the allotted amount appropriated for making up the amount in arrears falls short of the amount in arrears after the disposition on delinquency has been completed;
  2. Where the extinctive prescription of the rights to receive the farmland preservation charges has been completed;
  3. Where the estimated value of gross property which is the object of the disposition on delinquency leaves no room for remainder after appropriating it for covering the expenses for disposition on delinquency;
  4. Where it is recognized that there is no possibility for collection for the grounds prescribed by Presidential Decree, including the defaulter's death, having gone missing.
  (13) Where the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs has a person delegated with authority pursuant to Article 51 or a person entrusted with the affairs of operation and management of the Farmland Management Fund pursuant to Article 35(2) of the Korea Rural Community Corporation and Farmland Management Fund Act perform the affairs concerning imposition and collection of the farmland preservation charges, the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs shall pay fees to him or her, as prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008; Dec. 29, 2008; May 27, 2009; Jan. 17, 2012; Mar. 23, 2013; Jan. 20, 2015>
  (14) A person who operates and manages the Farmland Management Fund shall pay the amount after deducting fees prescribed in paragraph (13) from the farmland preservation charges he or she receives pursuant to paragraph (1) to the Farmland Management Fund. <Amended on May 27, 2009; Jan. 20, 2015>
  (15) The deadline and procedure for the payment of farmland preservation charges, and other necessary matters shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on May 27, 2009; Jan. 20, 2015>

Article 39 (Cancellation of permission to convert farmland) (1) Where a person who has obtained permission for farmland conversion under Article 34(1) or permission to temporarily use farmland for other purpose under Article 36 or has filed a report on farmland conversion under Article 35 or 43, a report on the temporary use of farmland for other purposes under Article 36-2, or a report on farmland improvement under Article 41-3 falls under any of the following cases, the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs, or the head of a Si/Gun/autonomous Gu may cancel the permission or order suspension of the related construction works, suspension of the operation, reduction of the scale of the business, modification of the project plan, and other necessary measures, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs; provided, where he or she falls under subparagraph 7, the relevant permission shall be canceled: <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008; May 27, 2009; Mar. 23, 2013; Jan. 20, 2015; Oct. 31, 2017; Jan. 2, 2024>
  1. Where it is proved that he or she has obtained the permission or has filed the report by fraud or other improper means;
  2. Where he or she violates the purpose or any condition of the permission;
  3. Where he or she modifies the project plan or project scale without obtaining permission or filing a report;
  4. Where he or she, having obtained the permission or having filed the report, has not commenced an intended project for farmland conversion for at least 2 years from the date the initial approval or report was granted or filed, including creation of building sites and installation of facilities, or has suspended the construction works for at least 1 year after having commenced the intended project for farmland conversion without good cause prescribed by Presidential Decree, including modification of the project plan related to the intended project for farmland conversion;
  5. Where he or she has not paid the farmland preservation charges;
  6. Where a person who has obtained permission or has filed the report applies for cancellation of the permission or withdraws the report;
  7. Where a person who obtained the permission has violated an order for measures under the main clause of this Article, including suspension of the related construction works.
  (2) Where a person who intends to convert farmland through consultation legally construed as the farmland conversion pursuant to other statutes fails to pay a farmland preservation charge after it was imposed, and to commence an intended project that causes the farmland conversion within 2 years, the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs may request the head of a relevant agency to revoke approval, permission, etc. related to such intended project. In such cases, the head of the relevant agency who receives a request for revocation shall comply with such request unless there is any compelling reason not to do so. <Added on Jan. 20, 2015>

Article 40 (Approval for alteration of use) (1) Where a person intends to use any farmland being used or used for an intended project for farmland conversion for another purpose within the period prescribed by Presidential Decree through any of the following procedures, he or she shall obtain approval from the head of a Si/Gun/autonomous Gu, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs: <Amended on Dec. 24, 2018>
  1. Permission for farmland conversion prescribed in Article 34(1);
  2. Consultation on the farmland conversion prescribed in Article 34(2)2;
  3. A report on farmland conversion under Article 35 or 43.
  (2) A person who intends to use any farmland converted to a site for facilities for which the farmland preservation charges are reduced or exempted as a site for facilities of which rate of reduction or exemption of the farmland preservation charges is different from that of the said site and who is subject to approval under paragraph (1) shall pay the corresponding farmland preservation charges, as prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 41 (Restrictions on changes of land category of farmland) (1) No farmland shall be changed to fall under any land category other than a field, rice field, or orchard except in any of the following cases: <Amended on Aug. 16, 2023; Jan. 2, 2024>
  1. Where permission for farmland conversion has been obtained under Article 34(1) or farmland has been converted under paragraph (2) of the same Article;
  2. Where farmland has been converted for the purpose described in Article 34(1)4;
  3. Where farmland is converted after a report on farmland conversion is filed under Article 35 or 43;
  4. Where farmland is changed to a site for land improvement facilities as defined in subparagraph 1b of Article 2 of this Act as a result of implementing a rural water development project for agricultural and fishing villages or an agricultural production infrastructure improvement project as defined in subparagraph 5a or b of Article 2 of the Agricultural and Fishing Villages Improvement Act;
  5. Where the head of a Si/Gun/autonomous Gu recognizes that the restoration of farmland is almost impossible because the form and quality of the farmland has been substantially changed due to natural disasters or other force majeure events.
  (2) Where the use of land is changed since the change in the form and quality of land has been completed on any of the grounds referred to in the subparagraphs of paragraph (1), the landowner shall file an application for the change of land category with the competent cadastral authority under subparagraph 18 of Article 2 of the Act on the Establishment and Management of Spatial Data within 60 days from the date such ground arises. <Added on Jan. 2, 2024>

Article 41-2 (Compliance with standards for farmland improvement) (1) A person who intends to improve farmland shall comply with the standards for farmland improvement (hereinafter referred to as "standards for farmland improvement") to achieve the objectives of farmland improvement, such as boosting productivity of farmland, and not to adversely affect the surrounding agricultural environment (including irrigation, drainage, ventilation, and farming operations of neighboring farmland) as a result of such farmland improvement.
  (2) Details concerning the standards for farmland improvement, including the following, shall be prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs:
  1. Scope of soil suitable for farmland improvement;
  2. Measures to prevent damage to neighboring farmland or facilities when farmland improvement;
  3. Other detailed standards related to dressing, filling, or cutting soil on farmland.
  [This Article Added on Jan. 2, 2024]

Article 41-3 (Reporting on farmland improvement) (1) Any person who intends to fill or cut soil, among persons intending to improve farmland, shall file a report thereon with the head of a Si/Gun/autonomous Gu, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs, and the same shall also apply where intending to modify any of the reported matters; provided, this shall not apply to any of the following cases:
  1. Where approval is granted for a development activity under Article 56 of the National Land Planning and Utilization Act;
  2. Where soil is filled or cut for a project directly implemented by the State or a local government as it is required for public interests;
  3. Where such filling or cutting is required for emergency measures for disaster recovery or disaster control;
  4. Where it is a minor act prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  (2) Where the head of a Si/Gun/autonomous Gu receives a report under paragraph (1), he or she shall review the details thereof, and shall accept the report if it is in compliance with this Act.
  [This Article Added on Jan. 2, 2024]

Article 42 (Restoration) (1) The Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs or the head of a Si/Gun/autonomous Gu may order a person who commits any of the following acts, the owner, occupant or manager of the relevant farmland to restore the farmland within a specified period: <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008; Mar. 23, 2013; Oct. 31, 2017; Jan. 2, 2024>
  1. Where he or she has converted farmland or has used it for another purpose without obtaining permission for farmland conversion under Article 34(1) or permission to temporarily use farmland for another purpose under Article 36;
  2. Where he or she has converted farmland or has used it for other purposes without filing a report on farmland conversion under Article 35 or 43 or without filing a report on the temporary use of farmland for other purposes under Article 36-2;
  3. Where permission has been canceled under Article 39;
  4. Where a person who filed a report on farmland conversion has violated an order for measures under Article 39.
  5. Where he or she improves farmland without complying with the standards for farmland improvement under Article 41-2;
  6. Where farmland soil has been filled or cut without any report thereon or report on modification under Article 41-3(1).
  (2) Where a person fails to restore farmland, in violation of the restoration order issued under paragraph (1), the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs or the head of a Si/Gun/autonomous Gu may restore such farmland by vicarious execution. <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008; Mar. 23, 2013>
  (3) The Administrative Vicarious Execution Act shall apply to the procedures of the vicarious execution under paragraph (2).

Article 42-2 (Corrective order) (1) The head of a Si/Gun/autonomous Gu may order a person who violates Article 32(1) or (2) or the owner, occupant, or manager of the relevant land to take corrective measures within a specified period.
  (2) Types of corrective measures referred to in paragraph (1), procedures therefor, and matters necessary for the implementation thereof shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  [This Article Added on Jan. 2, 2024]

Article 43 (Special cases concerning permission for farmland conversion) When a person obliged to obtain permission for farmland conversion under Article 34(1) intends to convert farmland falling under any of the following subparagraphs, he or she may convert the farmland after filing a report thereon with the head of a Si/Gun/autonomous Gu, as prescribed by Presidential Decree, notwithstanding Article 34(1) or 37(1): <Amended on Feb. 27, 2026>
  1. Farmland falling under Article 6(2)9-2;
  2. Farmland intended to be used as a site for facilities prescribed by Presidential Decree that are established within a rural specialized district under subparagraph 10 of Article 2 of the Act on Support for Rural Spatial Restructuring and Regeneration.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 27, 2009]

Article 43-2 (Designation of Zones on Farmland) (1) Where the head of a relevant administrative agency intends to designate or determine farmland as an area, district, zone, etc. to use such farmland for a particular purpose pursuant to other statutes, he or she shall consult thereon with the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs in advance according to the classification by type, size, etc. of farmland prescribed by Presidential Decree. The same shall also apply where he or she intends to change any of the matters already consulted on (excluding insignificant matters prescribed by Presidential Decree).
  (2) Matters necessary for the scope of consultation under paragraph (1), standards and procedures therefor and others shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  (3) The State or a local government shall neither designate single farmland as an area, district, zone, etc. related to the preservation of farmland nor restrict activities, in the absence of unavoidable circumstances.
  [This Article Added on Jan. 2, 2024]

SECTION 3 Farmland Committee

Article 44 (Establishment of farmland committee) A farmland committee shall be established in each Si/Gu/Eup/Myeon to efficiently manage the acquisition and use of farmland; provided, if the size of such farmland in the relevant area is not more than that prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs or if necessary for the efficient operation of a farmland committee, such farmland committee for each region may be established within the relevant administrative district, as prescribed by ordinance of the relevant Si/Gun.
  [This Article Added on Aug. 17, 2021]

Article 45 (Composition of farmland committee) (1) Each farmland committee shall be composed of not less than 10 but not more than 20 members, including 1 chairperson, who shall be elected among and by its members.
  (2) Any of the following persons shall serve as a member of each farmland committee:
  1. A person who engages in agricultural management in the relevant area;
  2. A person who is recommended by an agriculture-related institution or organization located in the relevant area;
  3. A person who is recommended by a non-profit, non-governmental organization defined in Article 2 of the Assistance for Non-Profit, Non-Governmental Organizations Act;
  4. A person with extensive knowledge of and experience in agriculture and farmland policies.
  (3) Where necessary for the efficient operation of a farmland committee, a subcommittee composed of up to 10 members shall be established therein.
  (4) Deliberation by a subcommittee shall be deemed deliberation by the relevant farmland committee.
  (5) Matters necessary for the operation of a farmland committee and its subcommittees, such as the term of office, appointment, and dismissal of a member, shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  [This Article Added on Aug. 17, 2021]

Article 46 (Functions of farmland committee) Each farmland committee shall perform the following functions:
  1. Matters regarding the examination of a qualification certificate for acquisition of farmland under Article 8(3);
  2. Verification of the status of implementing an intended project for farmland for which permission for farmland conversion is granted under Article 34(1);
  3. Participation in surveys on the ownership, etc. of farmland under Article 54(1);
  4. Other matters regarding the management of farmland prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs.
  [This Article Added on Aug. 17, 2021]

SECTION 3-2 Basic Policy for Farmland Management

Article 47 (Formulation of basic policy for farmland management) (1) The Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs shall formulate and implement a basic policy on the management of farmland (hereinafter referred to as the "basic policy") every 10 years and, where necessary, may review and update its contents every 5 years.
  (2) The basic policy shall include the following matters: <Amended on Jun. 16, 2026>
  1. The direction of policy measures concerning farmland management;
  2. The current status and future projections of farmland area;
  3. The target area of farmland to be managed; in such cases, the target food self-sufficiency ratio under Article 14(3)1 of the Framework Act on Agriculture, Rural Community and Food Industry shall be taken into consideration;
  4. Criteria for establishing the target area of farmland to be managed by a Special Metropolitan City, Integrated Special Metropolitan City, Metropolitan City, Special Self-Governing City, Do, or Special Self-Governing Province;
  5. Criteria for the designation and cancellation of agricultural promotion areas;
  6. Matters concerning the prevention of reductions in farmland area resulting from conversion of farmland and other causes;
  7. Matters concerning inspections of the implementation of the farmland management master plan and action plan under Article 48(1) and (2);
  8. Other matters prescribed by Presidential Decree as necessary for the management of farmland.
  (3) Where the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs intends to formulate or amend the basic policy, he or she shall, after hearing the opinions of the heads of local governments in advance and consulting with the heads of the relevant central administrative agencies, obtain advice from the Committee, undergo deliberation by the State Council, and obtain approval from the President; provided, this shall not apply where minor matters prescribed by Presidential Decree are amended. <Amended on Jun. 16, 2026>
  (4) For the purpose of formulating the basic policy, the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs may request the heads of the relevant central administrative agencies and the heads of local governments to submit necessary data. In such cases, the heads of the central administrative agencies and others requested to submit such data shall comply therewith unless there is a compelling reason not to do so.
  (5) Matters necessary for the formulation and implementation of the basic policy, other than those provided for in paragraphs (1) through (4), shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  [This Article Added on Jan. 23, 2024]

Article 48 (Formulation of farmland management master plan and action plan) (1) A Mayor/Do Governor shall formulate and implement a master plan on the management of farmland under his or her jurisdiction (hereinafter referred to as "master plan") every 10 years in accordance with the basic policy with the approval of the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs, and may review and update the details thereof every 5 years, if necessary. The same shall also apply where he or she intends to amend any important matter prescribed by Presidential Decree among the master plan.
  (2) The head of a Si/Gun/an autonomous Gu (excluding the head of an autonomous Gu where no farmland exists under his or her jurisdiction; hereafter the same shall apply in this Article) shall formulate and implement a detailed action plan on the management of farmland under his or her jurisdiction (hereinafter referred to as "action plan") every 5 years in accordance with the master plan with the approval of the Mayor/Do Governor. The same shall also apply where he or she intends to amend any important matter prescribed by Presidential Decree among the action plan.
  (3) The master plan and action plan shall include the following matters:
  1. The direction of policy measures concerning farmland management within the relevant jurisdiction;
  2. The current status and future projections of farmland area within the relevant jurisdiction;
  3. The target area of farmland to be managed within each jurisdiction;
  4. The designation and management of agricultural promotion areas within the relevant jurisdiction;
  5. The location and scale of areas within the relevant jurisdiction for which designation as agricultural promotion areas is deemed appropriate;
  6. Matters concerning the prevention of reductions in farmland area resulting from conversion of farmland and other causes within the relevant jurisdiction;
  7. Other matters prescribed by Presidential Decree as necessary for farmland management within the relevant jurisdiction.
  (4) Where a Mayor/Do Governor intends to formulate or amend the master plan, he or she shall, in advance, collect the opinions of the relevant head of a Si/Gun/autonomous Gu and experts, and hear the opinions of the relevant local council; provided, this shall not apply where minor matters prescribed by Presidential Decree are amended.
  (5) For the purpose of formulating the master plan, a Mayor/Do Governor may request the head of a Si/Gun/autonomous Gu to submit necessary data. In such cases, the head of the relevant Si/Gun or autonomous Gu requested to submit such data shall comply therewith unless there is a compelling reason not to do so.
  (6) Where the head of a Si/Gun/autonomous Gu intends to formulate or amend an action plan or present opinions on the master plan pursuant to paragraph (4), he or she shall, in advance, collect the opinions of residents and relevant experts and hear the opinions of the relevant local council, as prescribed by Presidential Decree; provided, this shall not apply where minor matters prescribed by Presidential Decree are amended.
  (7) Where a Mayor/Do Governor or the head of a Si/Gun/autonomous Gu obtains approval for the formulation or amendment of the master plan or action plan pursuant to paragraph (1) or (2), he or she shall publicly announce the contents thereof and make them available for public inspection, as prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  (8) Matters necessary for the formulation and implementation of the master plan or action plan, other than those provided for in paragraphs (1) through (7), shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  [This Article Added on Jan. 23, 2024]

SECTION 4 Farmland Ledgers

Article 49 (Preparing and keeping farmland ledgers) (1) To efficiently use and manage farmland by ascertaining the actual status of the ownership and use of the farmland, the head of a Si/Gu/Eup/Myeon shall prepare and keep a farmland ledger, as prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on Aug. 17, 2021>
  (2) The farmland ledger under paragraph (1) shall include the location, lot number, land category, area, owner, and information on lease of farmland, whether the farmland is in an agricultural promotion area, and other relevant matters. <Added on Aug. 17, 2021>
  (3) In order to prepare and arrange a farmland ledger under paragraph (1) or to ascertain the actual status of the use of farmland, the head of a Si/Gu/Eup/Myeon may, if necessary, require the owner of the farmland to report necessary matters or require a relevant public official to investigate the conditions thereof. <Amended on Aug. 17, 2021>
  (4) If the content of a farmland ledger is modified, the head of the Si/Gu/Eup/Myeon shall, without delay, arrange such modification in the farmland ledger. <Amended on Aug. 17, 2021>
  (5) Where matters to be recorded in a farmland ledger under paragraph (1) are recorded through an electronic data processing system, the electronic file of such farmland ledger (referring to a magnetic disc, magnetic tape, or other farmland ledger recorded and kept by means similar to the aforesaid ones) shall be deemed the farmland ledger under paragraph (1). <Amended on Aug. 17, 2021>
  (6) Matters necessary for the form, preparation, and management of the farmland ledger and for the electronic data processing system, etc. shall be prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs. <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008; Mar. 23, 2013; Aug. 17, 2021>
  [Title Amended on Aug. 17, 2021]

Article 49-2 (Application for change of information on use of farmland) Where any of the following reasons occurs, an owner or a lessee of farmland shall file an application for change to a farmland ledger with the head of a Si/Gu/Eup/Myeon within 60 days from the date the reason for such change occurs: <Amended on Jan. 2, 2024; Feb. 27, 2026>
  1. Where a lease agreement or gratuitous-loan-for-use agreement regarding farmland is concluded, changed, or canceled;
  2. Where production facilities for agricultural and stock farm products are installed on land under subparagraph 1b of Article 2;
  3. Where installing convenience facilities necessary for farming operations on land referred to in subparagraph 1c of Article 2;
  4. Where there exists any reason prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs.
  [This Article Added on Aug. 17, 2021]

Article 50 (Public perusal of farmland ledgers or issuance of certified copies thereof) (1) Upon receipt of a request for perusal of a farmland ledger or for issuance of a certified copy thereof, the head of a Si/Gu/Eup/Myeon shall make the farmland ledger available for perusal or shall issue a certified copy thereof, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs. <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008; Mar. 23, 2013; Aug. 17, 2021>
  (2) If a farmer or an agricultural corporation who conducts self-cultivation on his or her or its own farmland applies for a certificate of self-cultivation, the head of a Si/Gu/Eup/Myeon shall issue it, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs. <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008; Mar. 23, 2013>
  [Title Amended on Aug. 17, 2021]

CHAPTER V SUPPLEMENTARY PROVISIONS

Article 51 (Delegation and entrustment of authority) (1) The Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs may delegate part of his or her authority vested under this Act to the head of an affiliated agency, a Mayor/Do Governor, or the head of a Si/Gun/autonomous Gu, as prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008; Mar. 23, 2013; Aug. 17, 2021>
  (2) The Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs may entrust part of his or her affairs under this Act to the Korea Rural Community Corporation, an agriculture-related institution or organization, as prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008; Dec. 29, 2008; Mar. 23, 2013>
  (3) The Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs may, as prescribed by Presidential Decree, require a person entrusted with the affairs concerning operation and management of the Farmland Management Fund under Article 35 of the Korea Rural Community Corporation and Farmland Management Fund Act to perform the affairs of receiving farmland preservation charges under Articles 38(1) and 40(2) on his or her behalf. <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008; Dec. 29, 2008; Mar. 23, 2013>

Article 51-2 (Legal fiction as public official for purposes of applying penalty provisions) Members of the Committee and the Farmland Committee under Article 44 who are not public officials, and farmland investigators under Article 54-5, are deemed public officials for the purposes of applying Articles 127 and 129 through 132 of the Criminal Act. <Amended on Jun. 16, 2026>
  [This Article Added on Aug. 17, 2021]

Article 52 (Monetary award) The Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs may pay a monetary award, as prescribed by Presidential Decree, to a person who reports or files a complaint with the competent administrative authority or an investigative agency against a person falling under any of the following subparagraphs: <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008; Mar. 23, 2013; Oct. 31, 2017; Jun. 16, 2026>
  1. A person who has been issued with a qualification certificate for acquisition of farmland under Article 8(1) for the purpose of farmland ownership by fraud or other improper means, in violation of any restriction on farmland ownership under Article 6 or the maximum limits of farmland ownership under Article 7;
  1-2. A person who leases his or her own farmland or allows it to be used free of charge, in violation of Article 23(1);
  2. A person who has violated Article 32(1) or (2);
  3. A person who has converted farmland without obtaining permission for farmland conversion under Article 34(1) or a person who has obtained permission for farmland conversion under Article 34(1) by fraud or other improper means;
  4. A person who has converted farmland without filing a report under Article 35 or 43;
  5. A person who has used farmland for other purposes without obtaining permission for temporarily use of farmland for other purposes under Article 36(1);
  6. A person who has used farmland for other purposes without filing a report on the temporary use of farmland for other purposes under Article 36-2(1);
  7. A person who has used converted farmland for other purposes without obtaining approval therefor in violation of Article 40(1).

Article 53 (Special cases concerning restrictions on Acts with respect to parcel of farmland extended over agricultural promotion area and agricultural protection area) (1) Where a parcel of farmland is extended over both an agricultural promotion area and an agricultural protection area, and part of the farmland belonging to the agricultural promotion area is smaller than the scale prescribed by Presidential Decree, when the restriction on acts under Article 32 is applied to such part of the farmland, the provisions concerning agricultural protection areas shall be applied.
  (2) Where part of a parcel of farmland is extended over an agricultural promotion area and the area of the farmland belonging to the agricultural promotion area is smaller than the scale prescribed by Presidential Decree, Article 32(1) and (2) shall not be applied to the said part of the farmland.

Article 54 (Investigation into ownership of farmland) (1) The Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs, the head of a Si/Gun/autonomous Gu shall have public officials under his or her jurisdiction or farmland investigators under Article 54-5 (hereafter in this Article and Article 54-4 referred to as "farmland investigators") regularly conduct surveys on the actual status of farmland in order to verify facts concerning the ownership, transactions, use, or farmland conversion. <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008; Mar. 23, 2013; Aug. 17, 2021; Jun. 16, 2026>
  1. Deleted; <Aug. 17, 2021>
  2. Deleted; <Aug. 17, 2021>
  3. Deleted; <Aug. 17, 2021>
  4. Deleted; <Aug. 17, 2021>
  5. Deleted; <Aug. 17, 2021>
  6. Deleted. <Aug. 17, 2021>
  (2) The Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs or the head of a Si/Gun/autonomous Gu may request an owner or a lessee of farmland or a borrower under a gratuitous loan for use to submit necessary materials or state his or her opinion in order to ascertain facts regarding the ownership, transaction, use, conversion, etc. of farmland pursuant to paragraph (1). In such cases, the owner or lessee of farmland or the borrower under a gratuitous loan for use who is requested to submit materials or state his or her opinion shall cooperate in such request unless there is a compelling reason not to do so. <Added on Aug. 16, 2023>
  (3) An investigation under paragraph (1) shall be conducted at least once every year for farmland prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs, such as farmland for which a qualification certificate for acquisition of farmland is issued under Article 8 within a certain period. <Added on Aug. 17, 2021; Aug. 16, 2023>
  (4) The head of a Si/Gun/autonomous Gu shall conduct the survey under paragraph (1) and report the results thereof to the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs through the Mayor/Do Governor by March 31 of the following year. <Added on Aug. 17, 2021; Aug. 16, 2023; Jun. 16, 2026>
  (5) The Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs may disclose the results of the survey under paragraph (1) and the survey results reported pursuant to paragraph (4), as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs. <Added on Aug. 17, 2021; Aug. 16, 2023; Jun. 16, 2026>
  (6) A public official or farmland investigator conducting an inspection or survey pursuant to paragraph (1) shall carry identification verifying his or her authority and present it to the relevant persons. <Amended on Aug. 17, 2021; Aug. 16, 2023; Jun. 16, 2026>
  (7) Matters necessary for inspection, investigation, and identification referred to in paragraphs (1) and (3) shall be prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs. <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008; Mar. 23, 2013; Aug. 17, 2021; Aug. 16, 2023>
  (8) The Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs may subsidize the head of a Si/Gun/autonomous Gu to cover expenses incurred in conducting investigations under paragraph (1) within the budget. <Added on Aug. 17, 2021; Aug. 16, 2023>
  (9) The Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs, the head of a Si/Gun/autonomous Gu shall require the head of an Si/Gu/Eup/Myeon to reflect the results of the survey under paragraph (1) in the farmland ledger under Article 49. <Added on Jun. 16, 2026>

Article 54-2 (Management and operation of farmland information) (1) The Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs, the head of a Si/Gun/Gu, etc. may make a request for provision of data prescribed by Presidential Decree, such as electronic data on resident registration or real estate registration, from the head of an agency that manages the relevant data to use such data in the formulation of farmland-related policies, the preparation of farmland ledgers, etc., and the head of the agency in receipt of such request shall comply therewith except in extenuating circumstances. <Amended on Aug. 17, 2021>
  (2) The Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs may manage business information on agricultural management of agricultural business entities that is registered under Article 4 of the Act on Fostering and Supporting Agricultural and Fisheries Business Entities and data related to farmland under this Act in an integrated manner.
  (3) The Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs may establish and operate an information system to efficiently process various information necessary for affairs related to farmland and to digitalize affairs related to keeping records of and managing such information.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Apr. 13, 2021]

Article 54-3 (Provision of farmland information) Upon request of a bank under the Banking Act or any other financial institution prescribed by Presidential Decree, the head of a Si/Gun/autonomous Gu may provide information on notification of disposal of farmland under Article 10(2), orders to dispose of farmland under Article 11(1), the imposition of charges for compelling compliance under Article 63, or similar information, as prescribed by other statutes.
  [This Article Added on Aug. 17, 2021]

Article 54-4 (Access to land, etc.) (1) Where necessary for conducting any of the investigations specified in the following subparagraphs, the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs, the head of a Si/Gun/autonomous Gu, or the head of a Si/Gu/Eup/Myeon may have public officials under his or her jurisdiction (including officers and employees of the Korea Rural Community Corporation, agricultural-related institutions, or agricultural-related organizations where the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs entrusts any of the business affairs specified in the following subparagraphs to such corporation, institutions, or organizations pursuant to Article 51(2)) or farmland investigators (limited to cases where necessary for the investigation under subparagraph 3) enter another person's land, buildings, etc. (hereafter in this Article referred to as "land, etc."); provided, where a farmland investigator is caused to enter a place other than another person's land, a public official under the relevant jurisdiction shall accompany the farmland investigator: <Amended on Jun. 16, 2026>
  1. A fact-finding survey under Article 31-3(1);
  2. An investigation to ascertain the actual status of the preparation and arrangement of farmland ledgers or of farmland utilization under Article 49(3);
  3. An investigation to ascertain facts regarding the ownership, transaction, use, conversion, etc. of farmland under Article 54(1).
  (2) A person who intends to access land, etc. of other persons under paragraph (1) shall notify the owner, occupant, or manager of the relevant land, etc. (hereafter in this Article referred to as "interested person") of the date, time, and place of such access by mail, telephone, email, text message, etc.; provided, this shall not apply where an interested person is unknown.
  (3) No person shall access a housing site or the relevant land, etc. surrounded with a wall or fence before sunrise or after sunset without the consent of an interested person.
  (4) No interested person shall refuse or obstruct access under paragraph (1) without good cause.
  (5) A person who intends to access land, etc. of other persons under paragraph (1) shall carry identification verifying his or her authority and present it to interested persons.
  (6) Matters necessary for identification under paragraph (5) shall be prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs.
  [This Article Added on Aug. 16, 2023]

Article 54-5 (Employment of farmland investigators) (1) Where necessary to conduct an investigation under Article 54(1), the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs, the head of a Si/Gun/autonomous Gu may employ, during the relevant investigation period, residents of the Si, Gun, or autonomous Gu in which the farmland subject to investigation is located, or of an adjacent Si, Gun, or autonomous Gu, as farmland investigators.
  (2) The Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs, the head of a Si/Gun/autonomous Gu may pay remuneration and travel expenses to farmland investigators employed pursuant to paragraph (1) within the budget.
  [This Article Added on Jun. 16, 2026]

Article 54-6 (Duty of confidentiality) No person who is or was engaged in duties under Article 54 shall divulge any confidential information learned in the course of performing his or her duties to any other person or use such information for any purpose other than the performance of those duties.
  [This Article Added on Jun. 16, 2026]

Article 55 (Hearings) Where the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs or the head of a Si/Gun/autonomous Gu intends to engage in any of the following acts, he or she shall hold a hearing: <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008; Mar. 23, 2013>
  1. Notification of occurrence of an obligation to dispose of the farmland, etc. not used for agricultural management under Article 10(2);
  2. Cancellation of permission for farmland conversion under Article 39.

Article 56 (Fees) Any of the following persons shall pay fees, as prescribed by Presidential Decree: <Amended on Aug. 17, 2021>
  1. A person who applies for the issuance of a qualification certificate for acquisition of farmland under Article 8;
  2. A person who applies for permission under Article 34 or 36;
  3. A person who files a report on farmland conversion under Article 35 or 43;
  4. A person who applies for approval for diversion of use of farmland under Article 40;
  5. A person who applies for the issuance of a certified copy of a farmland ledger or of a certificate of self-cultivation under Article 50.

CHAPTER VI PENALTY PROVISIONS

Article 57 (Penalty provisions) A person who is issued with a qualification certificate for acquisition of farmland under Article 8(1) by fraud or other improper means for the purpose of owning farmland, in violation of restrictions on farmland ownership under Article 6 or of the maximum limit of farmland ownership under Article 7, shall be punished by imprisonment with labor for up to 5 years or by a fine not exceeding the amount equivalent to the price of the relevant land based on the officially assessed individual land price (hereinafter referred to as "land price").
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Aug. 17, 2021]

Article 58 (Penalty provisions) (1) A person who converts any farmland in an agricultural promotion area without having obtained permission for farmland conversion under Article 34(1) or obtains permission for farmland conversion by fraud or other improper means shall be punished by imprisonment with labor for not more than 5 years, or by a fine not exceeding the amount equivalent to the land price based on the officially assessed individual land price. <Amended on Aug. 17, 2021>
  (2) A person who converts any farmland outside an agricultural promotion area without having obtained permission for farmland conversion under Article 34(1) or obtains permission for farmland conversion by fraud or other improper means shall be punished by imprisonment with labor for not more than 3 years, or by a fine not exceeding the amount equivalent to 50/100 of the relevant land price. <Added on Aug. 17, 2021>
  (3) The imprisonment with labor and fines referred to in paragraphs (1) and (2) may be imposed concurrently. <Added on Aug. 17, 2021>

Article 59 (Penalty provisions) Any of the following persons shall be punished by imprisonment with labor for not more than 5 years or by a fine not exceeding 50 million won: <Amended on Dec. 15, 2014; Oct. 31, 2017; Dec. 24, 2018; Aug. 17, 2021>
  1. A person who violates Article 32(1) or (2);
  2. A person who uses farmland for another purpose without having obtained permission for temporarily use of farmland for other purposes prescribed in Article 36(1);
  3. A person who uses converted farmland for other purposes without having obtained approval, in violation of Article 40(1).

Article 60 (Penalty provisions) Any of the following persons shall be punished by imprisonment with labor for not more than 3 years or by a fine not exceeding 30 million won: <Amended on Jan. 2, 2024>
  1. A person who engages in any prohibited activity prescribed in Article 7-2;
  2. A person who converts farmland without filing a report prescribed in Article 35 or 43;
  3. A person who has used farmland for other purposes without filing a report on the temporary use of farmland for other purposes under Article 36-2(1).
  4. A person who improves farmland without complying with the standards for farmland improvement under Article 41-2;
  5. A person who fills or cuts farmland soil without any report thereon or report on modification under Article 41-3(1).
  [This Article Added on Aug. 17, 2021]
  [Previous Article 60 moved to Article 61 <Aug. 17, 2021>]

Article 60-2 (Penalty provisions) Any person who divulges or uses confidential information in violation of Article 54-6 shall be punished by imprisonment with labor for not more than 2 years or by a fine not exceeding 20 million won.
  [This Article Added on Jun. 16, 2026]

Article 61 (Penalty provisions) Any of the following persons shall be punished by a fine not exceeding 20 million won: <Amended on Jul. 20, 2015; Feb. 11, 2020; Aug. 17, 2021>
  1. A person who entrusts the agricultural management of his or her own farmland to another person, in violation of Article 9;
  2. A person who leases his or her own farmland or allows it to be used free of charge, in violation of Article 23(1);
  3. A person who fails to comply with an order to terminate a lease or a gratuitous loan for use under Article 23(2).
  [Moved from Article 60; previous Article 61 moved to Article 62 <Aug. 17, 2021>]

Article 62 (Joint penalty provisions) If a representative of a corporation, or an agent, employee, or other worker of a corporation or an individual commits any violation falling under Articles 57 through 60, Article 60-2, or Article 61 in connection with the business of the corporation or individual, not only shall the offender be punished, but also the corporation or individual shall be subject to the fine prescribed in the relevant Article; provided, this shall not apply where the corporation or individual has not neglected to exercise due care and supervision concerning the relevant business in order to prevent such violation. <Amended on Aug. 17, 2021; Jun. 16, 2026>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 27, 2009]
  [Moved from Article 61; previous Article 62 moved to Article 63 <Aug. 17, 2021>]

Article 63 (Charge for compelling compliance) (1) The head of a Si (referring to the head of a Si which has no Gus; hereafter in this Article the same shall apply)/Gun/Gu shall impose on any of the following persons a charge for compelling compliance equivalent to 25/100 of the greater of either of the farmland price appraised by an appraisal corporation, etc. under the Act on Appraisal and Certified Appraisers or the officially assessed individual land price under Article 10 of the Act on the Public Announcement of Real Estate Values (referring to the amount calculated based on the officially announced price of representative land under Article 8 of that Act, where the officially announced land price for the representative land does not exist): <Amended on Aug. 17, 2021; Aug. 16, 2023; Jan. 2, 2024; Jun. 16, 2026>
  1. A person who fails to comply with a disposal order issued under Article 11(1) (including cases under Article 12(2)) within a specified period without good cause prescribed by Presidential Decree, such as that consultation is underway upon request for purchase under the latter part of Article 11(2) after the disposal order has been issued;
  2. A person who fails to restore farmland within a reasonable period, despite the fact that the head of the Si/Gun/Gu specifies such period because the person to whom the restoration order was issued pursuant to Article 42 has failed to comply with the order within a compliance period.
  3. A person who fails to comply with a corrective order within a reasonable period, despite the fact that the head of a Si/Gun/Gu specifies such period because the person failed to comply with a corrective order issued pursuant to Article 42-2 within an initial compliance period.
  (2) The Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs or a Mayor/Do Governor shall impose a charge for compelling compliance equivalent to 25/100 of the higher of the following amounts on a person who, after receiving an order for disposition pursuant to Article 11(6) (including cases under Article 12(6)), fails to comply with the order for disposition within the designated period without good cause prescribed by Presidential Decree, such as where the person has requested purchase pursuant to the latter part of Article 11(2) and negotiations are in progress: the appraised value of the relevant farmland as appraised by an appraisal corporation, etc. under the Act on Appraisal and Certified Appraisers, or the officially assessed land price under Article 10 of the Act on the Public Announcement of Real Estate Values (where there is no officially assessed land price for the relevant land, the amount calculated based on the officially announced land price for the representative land under Article 8 of that Act). <Added on Jun. 16, 2026>
  (3) Before imposing a charge for compelling compliance under paragraph (1) or (2), the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs, a Mayor/Do Governor, the head of a Si/Gun/Gu shall notify the relevant person in writing in advance of the intention to impose and collect the charge for compelling compliance. <Amended on Jun. 16, 2026>
  (4) Where the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs, a Mayor/Do Governor, or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu intends to impose a charge for compelling compliance under paragraph (1) or (2), he or she shall do so by a document specifying the amount of the charge for compelling compliance, grounds for imposition, the deadline for payment, collecting agency, method of filing an objection, institution with which an objection may be filed, and other relevant matters. <Amended on Jun. 16, 2026>
  (5) The Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs, a Mayor/Do Governor, or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu may impose and collect a charge for compelling compliance under paragraph (1) or (2) once each year, commencing on the day following the expiration of the period for complying with a disposition order, restoration order, or corrective order, and continuing until such disposition order, restoration order, or corrective order is complied with. <Amended on Aug. 16, 2023; Jan. 2, 2024; Jun. 16, 2026>
  (6) Where a person who has received a disposition order under Article 11(1) (including cases under Article 12(2)), a disposition order under Article 11(6) (including cases under Article 12(6)), a restoration order under Article 42, or a corrective order under Article 42-2 complies with such disposition order, restoration order, or corrective order, the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs, a Mayor/Do Governor, or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu shall immediately discontinue the imposition of any new charge for compelling compliance; provided, any charge for compelling compliance already imposed shall be collected. <Amended on Aug. 16, 2023; Jan. 2, 2024; Jun. 16, 2026>
  (7) Any person who objects to a disposition imposing a charge for compelling compliance under paragraph (1) or (2) may file an objection with the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs, a Mayor/Do Governor, or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu within 30 days from the date on which he or she is notified of such disposition. <Amended on Jun. 16, 2026>
  (8) Where a person who has received a disposition imposing a charge for compelling compliance under paragraph (1) or (2) files an objection under paragraph (7), the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs, a Mayor/Do Governor, or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu shall, without delay, notify the competent court thereof, and the competent court so notified shall conduct judicial proceedings mutatis mutandis to a trial on administrative fines under the Non-Contentious Case Procedure Act. <Amended on Jun. 16, 2026>
  (9) Where a person neither files an objection within the period prescribed in paragraph (7) nor pays the charge for compelling compliance under paragraph (1) or (2) by the payment deadline, such charge shall be collected in accordance with the practices applicable to compulsory collection of national taxes or pursuant to the Act on the Collection of Local Administrative Penalty Charges. <Amended on Aug. 6, 2013; Mar. 24, 2020; Jun. 16, 2026>
  [Moved from Article 62 <Aug. 17, 2021>]

Article 64 (Administrative fines) (1) Any of the following persons shall be subject to an administrative fine not exceeding 5 million won:
  1. A person who falsely or improperly submits documents prescribed in Article 8(2);
  2. A person who falsely files an application under Article 49-2.
  (2) Any of the following persons shall be subject to an administrative fine not exceeding 3 million won: <Amended on Aug. 16, 2023; Jan. 2, 2024>
  1. A person who fails to file an application for the change of land category, in violation of Article 41(2);
  2. A person who fails to file an application under Article 49-2;
  3. A person who refuses, obstructs, or evades an investigation under Article 54(1);
  4. A person who refuses to submit materials or to state opinions, in violation of the latter part of Article 54(2), without good cause, or submits false materials or makes a false statement;
  5. A person who obstructs or refuses access without good cause, in violation of Article 54-4(4).
  (3) Administrative fines under paragraphs (1) and (2) shall be imposed and collected by administrative offices, as prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  [This Article Added on Aug. 17, 2021]

